// Runtime/ManagedSocketRuntime.maxon — the `__ManagedSocket` box: one OS socket, closed on drop.
//
// ⭐ **THE ORACLE IS `specs/managed-socket.md`, AND IT IS THE ONLY ORACLE AVAILABLE**, exactly as
// `ManagedFileRuntime`'s and `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`'s headers record for their families:
// `run_program compiler=csharp` crashes (`E9001`) on a standalone `__Managed*` call, so the committed cases
// are the whole of what can be asked of the reference.
//
// ⭐ **EVERY NATIVE LANE SERVES IT; `wasm32-wasi` REFUSES IT.** Which is which is one row —
// `TargetFacilities.targetProvidesFacility`'s `managedSocket` — and the refusal reaches a program at its own
// call site as `E3104 targetUnsupportedConstruct`, keyed on the `__ms_` PREFIX, so a method added later is
// gated by construction rather than by memory.
//
// ⚠ **ONE SURFACE, TWO Std GRAPHS, AND THE FORK IS THE RESOLVE** (`SocketResolveShape`): Windows and macOS
// call `getaddrinfo`, and the two libc-less Linux lanes run this file's own resolver over the file and
// socket ops it already has. Everything else in this file is target-neutral — the box, the guards, the
// ownership, the parks — which is what keeps the divergence to the one question the platforms differ on.
//
// ---
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE BOX IS ONE-LEVEL, WHICH IS `__ManagedFile`'s SHAPE AND DELIBERATELY NOT `__ManagedDirectory`'s.**
// A `__ManagedSocket` is an 8-byte box holding the raw OS SOCKET and nothing else. The directory family needs
// a second allocation because the OS WRITES THROUGH a 320-byte find record for the whole life of a search;
// a socket has no such record — every transfer hands the kernel the CALLER's buffer — so a second level would
// be an indirection with nothing in it. `specs/managed-socket.md`'s "Type Structure" states the single
// `_handle` field, and `SignatureIndex.managedSocketLayout` is what holds the front end's declared shape to
// the constants below.
//
// ⚠ **THE OPERATIONS ARE SYNCHRONOUS AND THE REFERENCE'S ARE NOT, AND THAT IS THIS TIER'S STANDING
// DIVERGENCE RATHER THAN A GAP IN THIS FILE.** The bootstrap runs `connect`/`send`/`recv` on an IOCP
// submission plus a pool of sync-fallback OS threads (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs`'s `__io_sync_op_net_*`),
// and registers each socket with the completion port. shv2 has no such pool: every `__ms_*` entry that makes
// a kernel call opens with `__gt_io_park`, the cooperative yield the `__mf_*`/`__md_*` bands already use, so
// the green thread genuinely suspends at the I/O point and others run — but the syscall is not overlapped
// with them. `GtIoParkName`'s header carries that argument in full for the filesystem bands; this family
// inherits it unchanged, which is also why the socket entries are NOT registered with an IOCP that would have
// nothing to complete into.
//
// ⚠ **"OPENS WITH" MEANS ONCE PER ENTRY POINT, NOT ONCE PER KERNEL CALL** — `emitIoPark`'s standing invariant,
// which this family did not hold until 2026-08-21 (`__ms_tcp_connect` parked four times; see the park site).
// `__ms_destruct` makes no kernel call of its own and parks through the `__ms_close` it delegates to, so the
// four parking entry points are connect / send / recv / close.

// ⭐ **THE BOX — its field offset and its size**, asserted against the registered `StructLayout`
// (`SignatureIndex.managedSocketLayout`) rather than agreeing with it by inspection, exactly as
// `ManagedFileHandleOffset`/`ManagedFileBoxBytes` are: the SHAPE is stated for the front end by that layout
// and written here by these constants, and two readers of one shape is the pair this project keeps finding
// drifted.
export let ManagedSocketHandleOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ManagedSocketBoxBytes = 8 as ParsedInt

// The value a handle SLOT carries when it holds no socket — `ManagedFileClosedHandle`'s twin, and zero for
// its reason: the box is allocated zeroed, so a box whose `socket()` never ran and one that has been closed
// carry the same value and ONE guard covers both.
//
// ⚠ **THE SLOT HOLDS `socket + HandleSlotBias`, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THAT SENTINEL TOTAL (MAC4).** A Win32
// `SOCKET` is never 0, so the bias buys this family nothing TODAY — but the guard it reads
// (`ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow`) is shared with two families where a descriptor of 0 is
// ordinary, so the ENCODING has to be one encoding. A POSIX `socket(2)` answers the lowest free descriptor
// exactly as `open(2)` does, so this is also the same latent defect one library over.
let ManagedSocketClosedHandle = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **`INVALID_SOCKET` IS ITS OWN CONSTANT EVEN THOUGH IT EQUALS `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE`.** Both are -1 and
// they are two different APIs' answers — `socket()` reports failure as `(SOCKET)(~0)`, `CreateFileA` as
// `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` — so naming this one here states a fact about the socket API instead of making a
// coincidence load-bearing across two families. It is the argument `emitErrnoClassifiedThrow`'s three flag
// parameters already make one type over.
let InvalidSocket = -1 as ParsedInt

// `SOCKET_ERROR` — what `send` and `recv` answer on failure. Its own constant beside `InvalidSocket` for
// that constant's reason: they are two different API answers that happen to share a value, and one name for
// both would make the coincidence load-bearing across the two.
let SocketError = -1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE IPv4/TCP PROTOCOL SELECTION — ONE HOME, TWO READERS, AND THE SECOND READER IS WHY IT MOVED HERE.**
// These three values are `socket()`'s three arguments (spelled by `StdToX64Conversion.lowerOsSocketCreate`)
// AND the three `addrinfo` fields `getaddrinfo` resolves against (`buildManagedSocketTcpConnect`'s `hints`,
// below). They must AGREE: resolve for `AF_INET` and then create an `AF_INET6` socket and `connect` fails
// with a family mismatch — a wrong answer with no diagnostic anywhere. The x64 lowering's own header used to
// say these lived there "and nowhere else"; that was true only while nothing above the target tier had to
// WRITE them into an OS structure, which building `hints` and `sockaddr_in` in Std now does.
//
// ⚠ **A POSIX OR WASI LANE STILL NAMES ITS OWN `socket()` ARGUMENTS — what it may not do is disagree with
// the `hints` this builder fills.** These three happen to be portable (`AF_INET` is 2, `SOCK_STREAM` is 1
// everywhere shv2 could target, and `IPPROTO_TCP` is IANA's 6); an IPv6 slice adds operands rather than
// editing them.
//
// ⚠ **`AfInet`, `SockStream` and `IpprotoTcp` HERE — AND `SockaddrInBytes` BELOW — are declared WITHOUT a
// `ParsedInt` suffix**, and that is load-bearing rather than sloppy: this file reads all four as `ParsedInt`
// (an `emitConst` value) while the x64 lowering reads all four as `MachineWord` (`int(0 to u64.max)`, a
// `movRegImm32` immediate). A literal type widens into both; stamping either one here would refuse the other
// reader. ⛔ The count is written where the members are NAMED, because this sentence said "the four" while
// standing over three declarations, with the fourth eight lines down under a comment of its own.
export let AfInet = 2
export let SockStream = 1
export let IpprotoTcp = 6

// `sizeof(struct sockaddr_in)` — the length `connect` is given (by the lowering) and the size this file
// ALLOCATES for the address it builds. One fact: a box smaller than the length handed to `connect` is a read
// past the end of an `__mm_alloc`'d block.
//
// ⚠ It is also the MINIMUM either POSIX kernel accepts: a shorter `addrlen` is refused with `EINVAL`
// (MEASURED on Darwin at 8), so the allocation size and the `connect` argument are not merely consistent by
// convention.
export let SockaddrInBytes = 16

// The `flags` argument of `send`/`recv` when there are none: no `MSG_OOB`, no `MSG_PEEK`.
// `specs/managed-socket.md` documents a plain stream send and receive, and a partial transfer is a normal
// answer rather than a flag.
//
// ⚠ **THE POSIX LANES PASS IT ON THE RECEIVE SIDE ONLY.** `MSG_NOSIGNAL` is a SEND-only flag there and its
// value is per-OS, so each isel names its own (`StdToArm64Conversion.DarwinMsgNoSignal`,
// `LinuxRuntime.LinuxMsgNoSignal`); Windows needs no counterpart because it has no SIGPIPE to suppress.
export let SendRecvNoFlags = 0

// `getaddrinfo`'s SERVICE argument, always NULL — see `StdOp.osSocketResolve`'s own header for why no port
// is ever formatted as a string for it. Read by both lanes that have a `getaddrinfo` to call.
export let SocketNoServiceName = 0

// ⭐⭐ **THE FIRST HALFWORD OF A `sockaddr_in`, AND DARWIN DOES NOT AGREE WITH THE OTHER TWO.** Windows and
// Linux begin the record with a 16-bit `sin_family`; the BSDs begin it with a one-byte `sin_len` and put
// `sin_family` in BYTE 1 (MEASURED with `offsetof` on this host: `sin_family` at 1 on Darwin, at 0 on an
// aarch64 Linux guest). ⇒ the halfword is `AF_INET` on two platforms and `sizeof(sockaddr_in) |
// (AF_INET << 8)` on Darwin, and this is the one place that difference is written down.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE WINDOWS SPELLING PASSES ON DARWIN BY ACCIDENT, WHICH IS EXACTLY WHY IT MAY NOT BE RELIED ON —
// MEASURED, WITH CONTROLS.** Writing 2 into byte 0 there sets `sin_len = 2` and leaves `sin_family` 0
// (`AF_UNSPEC`), which Darwin's `connect` accepts on an already-`AF_INET` socket: a probe connected with
// byte 1 zero, and connected again with byte 0 set to 30 (`AF_INET6`) — while ANY other value in byte 1 was
// refused with `EAFNOSUPPORT`. So byte 1 is the load-bearing one and byte 0 is ignored, and the record the
// Windows lane writes is right about neither.
function sockaddrFamilyHalfword(target Target) returns ParsedInt
	return match target.os 'os'
		windows to linux gives AfInet
		macos gives SockaddrInBytes + (AfInet * SockaddrFamilyByteScale)
		wasi gives AfInet
	end 'os'
end 'sockaddrFamilyHalfword'

// `1 shl 8` as a multiplier, so `sin_family` can be placed in BYTE 1 of the halfword above by an arithmetic
// the constant folder settles. Named rather than spelled 256 for `ByteTwoScale`'s reason: a bare power of
// two at a field position says nothing about which byte it encodes.
let SockaddrFamilyByteScale = 256

// `sizeof(WSADATA)` on Win64. `WSAStartup` fills it and nothing here reads it back — it is a scratch buffer
// the API demands, freed the moment the call returns.
let WsaDataBytes = 408 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **`struct addrinfo` ON Win64 — the HINTS this file writes and the RESULT it reads.** 48 bytes:
//
//   [0  .. 3]   ai_flags        [4  .. 7]   ai_family
//   [8  .. 11]  ai_socktype     [12 .. 15]  ai_protocol
//   [16 .. 23]  ai_addrlen      [24 .. 31]  ai_canonname
//   [32 .. 39]  ai_addr         [40 .. 47]  ai_next
//
// ⚠ `ai_addr` at 32 (0x20) is the one offset that is READ rather than written, and it is the bootstrap's own
// (`X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6270`, `MOV RAX, [RAX+0x20]`).
let AddrInfoBytes = 48 as ParsedInt

// The two WORDS this file writes into `hints`, named for the DWORD PAIR each one carries — `emitStoreDwordPair`
// composes two 32-bit fields into one 64-bit store, so an offset here names a word and not a field.
let AddrInfoFlagsFamilyOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let AddrInfoSocktypeProtocolOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
let AddrInfoAddrOffset = 32 as ByteOffset

// ⭐ **`struct sockaddr_in` — 16 bytes, and every field is NARROWER THAN A WORD.**
//
//   [0 .. 1]  sin_family (u16, host order)   [2 .. 3]  sin_port (u16, NETWORK order)
//   [4 .. 7]  sin_addr   (u32, network order) [8 .. 15] sin_zero
//
// The two READ offsets are used against the address `getaddrinfo` handed back; the WRITE goes through
// `emitSockaddrInWord`, which packs the first three into one store for the reason stated there.
let SockaddrAddrOffset = 4 as ByteOffset

// The single word `emitSockaddrInWord` packs `sin_family`, `sin_port` and `sin_addr` into — the whole of the
// WRITE side of this struct, which is why there is one offset here and not three.
let SockaddrFamilyPortAddrOffset = 0 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **2^32 AND 2^16 AS SCALES, BECAUSE THIS TIER HAS NO NARROW STORE.** `accessWidthFor` carries `byte` and
// `word64` and PANICS for everything between (deliberately — a narrow access needs the `as` cast so its
// SIGNEDNESS decides the extension), so a `u16` or `u32` FIELD cannot be stored on its own here. Every such
// field is written by composing it into the 64-bit word that contains it, and these are the two multipliers
// that place a field at byte 2 and at byte 4 of that word. Named rather than spelled `65536`/`4294967296` at
// five sites: a bare power of two at a shift says nothing about which field offset it encodes.
let ByteTwoScale = 65536 as ParsedInt
let ByteFourScale = 4294967296 as ParsedInt

// ⚠ **THE TEST-NET-1 SENTINEL, AND IT IS THE REFERENCE'S BEHAVIOUR RATHER THAN A TEST ACCOMMODATION.**
// `192.0.2.1` is RFC 5737's documentation range: it is guaranteed to route nowhere, so a real `connect` to it
// does not get refused — it STALLS for the OS's multi-second SYN timeout (~21 s on Windows). ALL FOUR
// reference backends short-circuit it (`maxon-sharp` x64 `X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6277` and arm64
// `ARM64CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:4730`; `maxon-selfhosted`'s `X64Backend.maxon:10744` `TestNet1NetOrder` and its
// wasm backend), and v1 states the reason in so many words: *"does not stall for the multi-second OS connect
// timeout"*. `specs/managed-socket.md`'s `connect-refused` case connects to exactly this address, and the
// spec suite's per-case budget is two seconds.
//
// The value is the address AS `sin_addr` HOLDS IT — network byte order, so `192.0.2.1` reads `0x010200C0`
// little-endian-first. It is compared against the resolved `sin_addr` word, never against a dotted string.
let TestNet1NetOrder = 0x010200C0 as ParsedInt

// The three "the call succeeded" readings. `WSAStartup` and `getaddrinfo` answer a plain int where 0 is
// success (`getaddrinfo` returns an EAI_* code otherwise), and `connect` answers 0 or `SOCKET_ERROR`.
let WinsockCallOk = 0 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **THE `__ManagedSocketError` ORDINALS, AS ERROR FLAGS (`ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`)** — the
// `ManagedFileRuntime`/`ManagedDirectoryRuntime` discipline, and the ordinals are positional facts about the
// case order of the enum DECLARED at `stdlib/Builtins.maxon:163-172`. `verifyManagedSocketOrdinals` below is
// what holds these literals to that declaration.
//
// ⚠ **`bufferOutOfBounds` IS ORDINAL 0.** It reads like the rare edge case and it sits FIRST, so a
// transcription that "tidied" the order into failure-severity order would silently re-point every other flag
// by one — with no compile error, because a flag is just a number.
//
// ⚠ `connectionClosed` (5) has no flag here because nothing THROWS it: `specs/managed-socket.md` documents
// `recv` as *"Returns 0 if the peer closed gracefully"*, so a graceful close is a plain 0 RETURN and the case
// is the CALLER's to raise (`stdlib/TcpClient.maxon:69` does exactly that). The case is DECLARED — the
// ordinals after it depend on its slot — and deliberately unthrown, which is why its ORDINAL is here even
// though its flag is not, and why `claimManagedSocketOrdinals` states it as `neverThrown` rather than
// leaving a gap a corpus reorder could move in silence.
let ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal = 0 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal = 1 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal = 2 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal = 3 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal = 4 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketConnectionClosedOrdinal = 5 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal = 6 as ParsedInt
let ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsFlag = ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketResolveFailedFlag = ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag = ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketSendFailedFlag = ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketRecvFailedFlag = ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias
let ManagedSocketClosedFlag = ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

// The `__ms_` prefix that identifies EVERY socket runtime entry point — the one test `scanRuntimeUsage` reads
// to set `usesManagedSocket`, the one `SemanticCheck` reads for E3104, and the one its yield roster reads.
let ManagedSocketRuntimePrefix = b"__ms_"

// ⭐ **THE FIVE ENTRY POINTS.** Four name a `__ManagedSocket` method — the surface BOTH references declare
// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/2-Parser.cs:1574-1583`) — and `__ms_destruct` is the compiler-INSERTED one: the drop
// a box takes when its last owner goes away (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`), which is the whole of
// the RAII half.
//
// ⚠ They are the ENTRY points and not every function this file installs: a lane running the built-in
// resolver carries ten more, all `__ms_`-prefixed and none reachable from source (see that section's own
// header). The prefix is what puts them inside `isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee`, and therefore inside the
// drift guard's walk.
export let ManagedSocketTcpConnectName = b"__ms_tcp_connect"
export let ManagedSocketSendFromName = b"__ms_send_from"
export let ManagedSocketRecvName = b"__ms_recv"
export let ManagedSocketCloseName = b"__ms_close"
export let ManagedSocketDestructName = b"__ms_destruct"

// ⭐ **WHICH `__ManagedSocket` CALLEES THROW** — the composer behind `GtRuntime.isThrowingRuntimeCallee`.
// Held to the builders by the bidirectional drift guard in `installManagedSocketRuntime`, copied from
// `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated reason: a predicate and a `throws` stamp are one fact in two
// places, and this project keeps finding that pair drifted.
export function isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	// ⚠ **THE TWO ABSENTEES ARE EACH ABSENT FOR A STATED REASON, not by oversight.**
	//   • `__ms_close` — `specs/managed-socket.md` documents `close()` as *"Idempotent. Also called
	//     automatically via destructor. Does not throw."*; naming it here would make a `try` REQUIRED on a
	//     call that cannot fail (E3055).
	//   • `__ms_destruct` — a DROP has no caller to hand an error to; it runs at a scope exit nobody wrote.
	return callee.equals(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName) or callee.equals(ManagedSocketSendFromName) or callee.equals(ManagedSocketRecvName)
end 'isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **WHAT THE USER ACTUALLY WROTE, FOR THE DIAGNOSTICS THAT QUOTE A CALLEE BACK AT THEM** —
// `managedDirectorySourceMethodName`'s twin, and its header carries the argument in full: an author never
// writes `__ms_recv`, so E3057 ("throwing function requires try") and E3059 must name the method they did
// write. It is a map onto the PARSER's own constants, not a second spelling, and it is asked only behind
// `isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee` — so the panic states a case nothing can reach rather than answering
// it. `installManagedSocketRuntime`'s drift guard asks it for every throwing `__ms_` builder it installs, so
// a missing arm fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`.
export function managedSocketSourceMethodName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName) 'tcpConnect'
		return ManagedSocketTcpConnectMethod
	end 'tcpConnect'
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketSendFromName) 'sendFrom'
		return ManagedSocketSendFromMethod
	end 'sendFrom'
	if callee.equals(ManagedSocketRecvName) 'recv'
		return ManagedSocketRecvMethod
	end 'recv'
	panic("managedSocketSourceMethodName: no source-method arm for '{String.from(callee)}'. EITHER it is not a throwing `__ManagedSocket` callee — this map is asked ONLY behind `isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee`, so nothing else can reach an E3057 — OR that predicate gained a name and this map did not, in which case add an arm answering the parser's own `ManagedSocket*Method` constant. `installManagedSocketRuntime`'s drift guard asks this for every throwing `__ms_` builder it installs, so the second case fails at INSTALL and never in front of a user's missing `try`")
end 'managedSocketSourceMethodName'

// ⭐ **THE ERROR TYPE A SOCKET CALLEE THROWS — the WHAT to the predicate's THAT**, and the twin of
// `managedFileThrows`/`managedDirectoryThrows`. `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf` answers with it for a callee no
// file declares, which is what types an `otherwise (e)` binding and therefore what makes `match e` see an
// enum instead of a scalar; the three throwing builders stamp it as their `throws` clause. ONE speller for
// one fact.
export function managedSocketThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(ManagedSocketErrorTypeName))
end 'managedSocketThrows'

// The live OS socket of a box, or this family's `closed` throw — `ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow`
// with THIS family's three fixed facts supplied. Every `__ManagedSocket` caller throws
// `__ManagedSocketError.closed` from a box whose handle sits at `ManagedSocketHandleOffset`, so those three
// are a per-SOCKET fact rather than a per-call one; this is what keeps the triple out of `sendFrom` and `recv`.
function emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, join IrBlock, box ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: join, record: box, handleOffset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, closedSentinel: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, closedFlag: ManagedSocketClosedFlag, ids: ids)
end 'emitLiveSocketOrThrow'

// ⭐ **TWO 32-BIT FIELDS SHARING ONE 64-BIT SLOT, WRITTEN AS ONE CONSTANT STORE.** `low` lands at `offset`
// and `high` at `offset + 4`. It exists because this tier has NO 4-byte store at all (see `ByteFourScale`),
// so a DWORD field is only reachable through the word that contains it — and doing that inline would put the
// "+4 is the high half" arithmetic at every `hints` field instead of once.
//
// ⚠ Both values are COMPILE-TIME here, so the packing is folded into a single `emitConst` rather than emitted
// as a shift and an or.
function emitStoreDwordPair(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, offset ByteOffset, low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let packed = emitConst(module, block: block, value: low + (high * ByteFourScale), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: offset, value: packed)
end 'emitStoreDwordPair'

// ⭐⭐ **THE `sockaddr_in`'s FIRST WORD — `sin_family`, `sin_port` AND `sin_addr` PACKED INTO ONE STORE.**
// The three fields occupy bytes 0..7 of a 16-byte address (`sin_zero` fills 8..15 and the block is allocated
// zeroed), and none of them is a word wide — so composing them is not an optimization, it is the only form
// this tier can write them in.
//
// ⚠ **`sin_port` IS NETWORK BYTE ORDER AND `sin_family` IS NOT**, which is the one asymmetry in the record
// and the one this function exists to state once. `htons` is the byte swap of the low 16 bits; `sin_addr`
// arrives ALREADY in network order (it is copied straight out of the `sockaddr` `getaddrinfo` filled), so it
// is placed and never swapped.
//
// ⚠ The FAMILY half is the caller's, because it is the one field whose byte position is per-platform — see
// `sockaddrFamilyHalfword`, which carries the measurement.
function emitSockaddrInWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, port ValueId, ip ValueId, familyHalfword ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	// htons: `((port and 0xFF) shl 8) or ((port shrLogical 8) and 0xFF)`.
	let lowMask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0xFF, ids: ids)
	let byteBits = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 8, ids: ids)
	let lowByte = emitBin(module, block: block, a: port, b: lowMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let lowMoved = emitBin(module, block: block, a: lowByte, b: byteBits, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let highShifted = emitBin(module, block: block, a: port, b: byteBits, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let highByte = emitBin(module, block: block, a: highShifted, b: lowMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let netPort = emitBin(module, block: block, a: lowMoved, b: highByte, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)

	// The family halfword at bytes 0..1, `sin_port` at byte 2, `sin_addr` at byte 4.
	let family = emitConst(module, block: block, value: familyHalfword, ids: ids)
	let portScale = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteTwoScale, ids: ids)
	let portPlaced = emitBin(module, block: block, a: netPort, b: portScale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let familyAndPort = emitBin(module, block: block, a: family, b: portPlaced, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let addrScale = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteFourScale, ids: ids)
	let addrPlaced = emitBin(module, block: block, a: ip, b: addrScale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: familyAndPort, b: addrPlaced, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSockaddrInWord'

// ==========================================================================
// WHICH RESOLVER A LANE HAS — the one fact this family forks the Std graph on
// ==========================================================================

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO WAYS A PLATFORM CAN TURN A HOST NAME INTO AN IPv4 ADDRESS, ASKED ONCE AT INSTALL.** It is
// `CommandLineRuntime.CommandLineShape`'s arrangement exactly, and for the same reason: each answer builds a
// COMPLETE Std graph, so nothing downstream of the fork ever asks which target it is compiling for.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE FORK IS FORCED BY THE ABSENCE OF A LIBRARY, NOT BY A MISSING LOWERING.** `getaddrinfo` is a
// libc FUNCTION — it reads `/etc/nsswitch.conf`, consults `/etc/hosts`, and speaks DNS to whatever
// `/etc/resolv.conf` names — and shv2's Linux lanes are raw static images that link no libc at all. There
// is no syscall to point `StdOp.osSocketResolve` at, in either arch's table, so those lanes cannot have a
// different LOWERING of the same op; they need a different GRAPH.
//
// ⚠ **WHY THE BUILT-IN RESOLVER IS Std IR RATHER THAN A PER-LANE CHUNK.** A resolver is a text scan, two
// file reads and a DNS exchange — several hundred instructions of loops — and a hand-assembled chunk would
// be that written TWICE, once per Linux arch, with two chances to get a label-length check wrong. Written
// here it is one body compiled by the isel that already lowers every op it uses, which is the same
// arrangement that lets `mrt_host_proc_reap` serve both POSIX lanes from one place.
enum SocketResolveShape
	// Windows and macOS: `getaddrinfo` fills a linked list of `struct addrinfo` and the graph reads the
	// first entry's `ai_addr`. ⚠ **THE `ai_addr` OFFSET IS THE SAME 32 ON BOTH, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER
	// THAN ASSUMED** — Darwin orders `ai_canonname` before `ai_addr` exactly as Win64 does (`offsetof` on
	// this host: 24 and 32), while LINUX REVERSES THEM (`ai_addr` at 24). So this shape's single
	// `AddrInfoAddrOffset` is correct for both of its lanes and would be wrong for the lane that does not
	// use it — which is one more reason the fork is a graph and not an offset table.
	addrInfoList
	// The Linux lanes: this file's own `__ms_resolve4`, over the existing file and socket ops.
	builtinResolver
end 'SocketResolveShape'

// ⚠ **WASI ANSWERS `addrInfoList` AND THAT IS A SHAPE ANSWER, NOT A CAPABILITY CLAIM** —
// `commandLineShape`'s own note, one family over. `wasi:sockets`'s `ip-name-lookup` answers a LIST of
// addresses, so the shape really is that one; what that lane lacks is any lowering at all for the band, and
// `targetProvidesFacility` is where that refusal lives. A shape function that lied about the shape to
// express a capability would put the same fact in two places.
function socketResolveShape(target Target) returns SocketResolveShape
	return match target.os 'os'
		windows gives SocketResolveShape.addrInfoList
		linux gives SocketResolveShape.builtinResolver
		macos gives SocketResolveShape.addrInfoList
		wasi gives SocketResolveShape.addrInfoList
	end 'os'
end 'socketResolveShape'

// ⭐ **RESOLVE `host` INTO A NETWORK-ORDER `sin_addr`, OR ROUTE TO `resolveFailed`.** Both shapes leave the
// answer in a value the `resolved` block may read — `resolve` is the only edge into it, so a value defined
// there is simply in scope (the dominance argument `emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow` makes for its own) — and
// both RELEASE the host cstring on every edge they take, which is what keeps that scratch out of the
// caller's four-allocation ledger.
function emitResolveHostIp(module StdModule, resolve IrBlock, resolved IrBlock, resolveFailed IrBlock, host ValueId, shape SocketResolveShape, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match shape 'shape'
		addrInfoList gives emitResolveThroughAddrInfo(module, resolve: resolve, resolved: resolved, resolveFailed: resolveFailed, host: host, ids: ids)
		builtinResolver gives emitResolveThroughBuiltinResolver(module, resolve: resolve, resolved: resolved, resolveFailed: resolveFailed, host: host, ids: ids)
	end 'shape'
end 'emitResolveHostIp'

// `getaddrinfo(host, NULL, hints, out)`, then the first result's `sin_addr`.
//
// `hints` — 48 bytes, allocated ZEROED, so only the three fields `getaddrinfo` reads are written. They are
// DWORDs at 4/8/12, two to a word (see `emitStoreDwordPair`): `ai_flags`+`ai_family` in the first,
// `ai_socktype`+`ai_protocol` in the second.
function emitResolveThroughAddrInfo(module StdModule, resolve IrBlock, resolved IrBlock, resolveFailed IrBlock, host ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let hints = emitRawAlloc(module, block: resolve, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: resolve, value: AddrInfoBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreDwordPair(module, block: resolve, record: hints, offset: AddrInfoFlagsFamilyOffset, low: 0, high: AfInet, ids: ids)
	emitStoreDwordPair(module, block: resolve, record: hints, offset: AddrInfoSocktypeProtocolOffset, low: SockStream, high: IpprotoTcp, ids: ids)

	// The out word holds the `addrinfo*` the OS allocates. Zeroed by `__mm_alloc`, so a failed resolve leaves
	// a NULL nothing reads.
	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: resolve, ids: ids)
	let resolveResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(resolve, op: StdOp.osSocketResolve(resolveResult, hostPtrId: host, hintsPtrId: hints, outPtrId: out))
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolve, ptr: hints, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolve, ptr: host, ids: ids)
	let resolveOk = emitConst(module, block: resolve, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let didResolve = emitCmp(module, block: resolve, a: resolveResult, b: resolveOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resolve, op: StdOp.condBranch(didResolve, thenBlockId: resolved.id, elseBlockId: resolveFailed.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: resolveFailed, ptr: out, ids: ids)

	// The first result's address, then the list goes back to the OS. `sin_addr` is a DWORD, so it is read as
	// the word containing it and masked — `emitLoadDword`'s reason, and the four bytes above it are `sin_zero`,
	// inside the same 16-byte `sockaddr`.
	let addrInfo = emitLoadField(module, block: resolved, record: out, offset: Win32OutWordAnswerOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolved, ptr: out, ids: ids)
	let sockaddr = emitLoadField(module, block: resolved, record: addrInfo, offset: AddrInfoAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	let ip = emitLoadDword(module, block: resolved, record: sockaddr, offset: SockaddrAddrOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(resolved, op: StdOp.osSocketFreeAddrInfo(addrInfo))

	return ip
end 'emitResolveThroughAddrInfo'

// `__ms_resolve4(host)` — one call, answering the address or `ResolveNoAddress`. The host cstring is
// released on the caller's side of the branch because this shape has no OS-owned list to release and
// therefore nothing else to do on either edge.
function emitResolveThroughBuiltinResolver(module StdModule, resolve IrBlock, resolved IrBlock, resolveFailed IrBlock, host ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let ip = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(resolve, op: StdOp.call(ip, callee: ManagedSocketResolveName, args: argsOf(host)))
	emitRawFree(module, block: resolve, ptr: host, ids: ids)
	let noAddress = emitConst(module, block: resolve, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)
	let didResolve = emitCmp(module, block: resolve, a: ip, b: noAddress, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resolve, op: StdOp.condBranch(didResolve, thenBlockId: resolved.id, elseBlockId: resolveFailed.id))

	return ip
end 'emitResolveThroughBuiltinResolver'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_tcp_connect(hostRec, port) -> (box, flag)` — RESOLVE, THEN CONNECT, OR THROW.**
//
// The sequence is `__io_sync_op_net_connect`'s (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6213-6350`),
// which is the reference that WORKS:
//
//   WSAStartup(2.2)  →  fill a 48-byte `hints` for IPv4/TCP  →  getaddrinfo(host, NULL, hints, out)
//   →  read `sin_addr` out of the first result's `ai_addr`  →  freeaddrinfo
//   →  the TEST-NET-1 short circuit  →  socket()  →  build a `sockaddr_in`  →  connect()
//
// ⚠ **EVERY FAILURE IS `resolveFailed` OR `connectFailed`, AND THAT IS A CONTRACT THE SPEC PINS RATHER THAN
// a convenience.** Both committed cases write `default panic("unreachable: tcpConnect reports only
// resolveFailed or connectFailed")`, so a third variant escaping this builder is an ABORT in a user's
// program. `getaddrinfo` answering non-zero is the resolve; **everything else — a `WSAStartup` that fails, a
// `socket()` that fails, a `connect()` that is refused — is `connectFailed`**, because with no Winsock and no
// socket there is no connection, which is exactly what that variant names.
//
// ⚠ **THE SCRATCH COPIES ARE FREED ON EVERY EXIT, THE THROWING ONES INCLUDED.** Four allocations pass through
// this function — the host cstring, the `WSADATA`, the `hints`, the `addrinfo*` out word — and each is
// released before the block that could leave. The `addrinfo` LIST is the OS's and goes back through
// `freeaddrinfo`, never through `__mm_free`.
function buildManagedSocketTcpConnect(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketTcpConnectName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var init = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msinit")
	var noWinsock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnowsa")
	var resolve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msresolve")
	var resolveFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnsfail")
	var resolved = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msaddr")
	var refused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrefused")
	var create = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mssock")
	var noSocket = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnosock")
	var dial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdial")
	var dialFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msconnfail")
	var connected = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let hostRec = 0 as ValueId
	let port = 1 as ValueId

	// The host arrives as a `__ManagedMemory` record whose buffer carries NO terminator, and `getaddrinfo`
	// takes an `LPCSTR` — `emitPathToCString`'s whole argument, one family over, and the scratch is this
	// function's to release.
	let host = emitPathToCString(module, func: func, cur: entry, join: init, pathRec: hostRec, ids: ids)

	// Winsock must be initialized before any other ws2_32 entry point answers. The `WSADATA` is write-only to
	// us: it is freed the moment the call returns.
	let wsaData = emitRawAlloc(module, block: init, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: init, value: WsaDataBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let startupResult = ids.mint()

	// ⛔ **`WSAStartup` IS NOT A PARK SITE.** It is process-cached behind `__net_wsa_init` in both references
	// and returns without touching the network on every call after the first, so a park here would report a
	// blocking operation that does not block. The two that DO are `getaddrinfo` and `connect()`, and each has
	// its own park below — see `emitIoPark` for why the unit is the blocking operation rather than the
	// function.
	module.appendOp(init, op: StdOp.osSocketStartup(startupResult, wsaDataPtrId: wsaData))
	emitRawFree(module, block: init, ptr: wsaData, ids: ids)
	let startupOk = emitConst(module, block: init, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let winsockReady = emitCmp(module, block: init, a: startupResult, b: startupOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(init, op: StdOp.condBranch(winsockReady, thenBlockId: resolve.id, elseBlockId: noWinsock.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: noWinsock, ptr: host, ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: noWinsock, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **PARK 1 OF 2 — THE DNS RESOLVE.** The resolve is the first of this function's two genuinely
	// blocking operations: it goes to a resolver over the network and can take longer than the connect that
	// follows it. The bootstrap gives it a park of its own by giving it a SYNC OP of its own —
	// `SyncOpDnsResolve`, which reports under the `net_connect` operation name
	// (`maxon-sharp/Compiler/MLIR/X86CodeEmitter.Runtime.cs:6754`) — so the pair a resolve prints is spelled
	// `[net_connect]` there too, and this is the same pair rather than a new spelling.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PARK IS HERE AND NOT INSIDE EITHER SHAPE, BECAUSE IT IS ABOUT THE OPERATION AND NOT ABOUT THE
	// MECHANISM.** Both shapes block the green thread for a network round trip; that is what a park reports,
	// and `emitIoPark`'s once-per-entry-point invariant would be broken by a second one inside the resolver.
	emitIoPark(module, block: resolve, operation: GtIoOperation.netConnect, ids: ids)
	let ip = emitResolveHostIp(module, resolve: resolve, resolved: resolved, resolveFailed: resolveFailed, host: host, shape: socketResolveShape(target), ids: ids)
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: resolveFailed, flag: ManagedSocketResolveFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	let testNet = emitConst(module, block: resolved, value: TestNet1NetOrder, ids: ids)
	let isTestNet = emitCmp(module, block: resolved, a: ip, b: testNet, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resolved, op: StdOp.condBranch(isTestNet, thenBlockId: refused.id, elseBlockId: create.id))

	// Nothing has been allocated or opened on this edge — see `TestNet1NetOrder` for why the connect is not
	// attempted at all.
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: refused, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	let socket = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(create, op: StdOp.osSocketCreate(socket))
	let invalidConst = emitConst(module, block: create, value: InvalidSocket, ids: ids)
	let socketFailed = emitCmp(module, block: create, a: socket, b: invalidConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(create, op: StdOp.condBranch(socketFailed, thenBlockId: noSocket.id, elseBlockId: dial.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: noSocket, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **PARK 2 OF 2 — THE TCP CONNECT.** `connect()` waits for a handshake with a host that may never
	// answer; it is the second blocking call and it gets the second park. `socket()` between the two does not:
	// it allocates a descriptor and returns.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE TEST-NET-1 EDGE NEVER REACHES HERE, AND THAT IS WHY `async-tcp.md`'s TWO TRACE CASES STILL PIN
	// ONE PAIR.** `192.0.2.1` short-circuits at `resolved` (see `TestNet1NetOrder`), so those programs perform
	// the resolve and no connect — one blocking operation, one pair. Both references answer identically for
	// the same reason: the bootstrap's `-2` sentinel comes back from the DNS sync op and
	// `maxon_net_tcp_connect` returns before its ConnectEx phase. A host that RESOLVES and then connects prints
	// two, which is what `specs/http-client.md` pins.
	emitIoPark(module, block: dial, operation: GtIoOperation.netConnect, ids: ids)
	let addr = emitRawAlloc(module, block: dial, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: dial, value: SockaddrInBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let addrWord = emitSockaddrInWord(module, block: dial, port: port, ip: ip, familyHalfword: sockaddrFamilyHalfword(target), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: dial, record: addr, offset: SockaddrFamilyPortAddrOffset, value: addrWord)
	let dialResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(dial, op: StdOp.osSocketConnect(dialResult, sockId: socket, addrPtrId: addr))
	emitRawFree(module, block: dial, ptr: addr, ids: ids)
	let dialOk = emitConst(module, block: dial, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let didConnect = emitCmp(module, block: dial, a: dialResult, b: dialOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dial, op: StdOp.condBranch(didConnect, thenBlockId: connected.id, elseBlockId: dialFailed.id))

	// A refused connect leaves an OPEN socket, which no box will ever own — so it is closed here or it leaks
	// for the process lifetime, invisibly to the exit-101 gate because a socket is not memory.
	module.appendOp(dialFailed, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	emitErrorReturn(module, block: dialFailed, flag: ManagedSocketConnectFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	// The box: one 8-byte slot holding the socket, allocated through `__mm_alloc` so the ordinary ownership
	// machinery drops it — and the drop it takes is `__ms_destruct`, which CLOSES the socket before freeing
	// the memory (`SignatureIndex.managedNameDropCallee`). That is the whole RAII half of this type.
	let box = emitRawAlloc(module, block: connected, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: connected, value: ManagedSocketBoxBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreHandleSlot(module, block: connected, record: box, handleOffset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, handle: socket, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: connected, value: box, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketTcpConnect'

// `__ms_send_from(box, arrRec, offset, length) -> (bytesSent, flag)` — send `length` bytes starting at
// `offset` in the managed buffer.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`offset + length > capacity` THROWS `bufferOutOfBounds`; IT IS NOT CLAMPED.** That is the spec's own
// contract (*"Throws `__ManagedSocketError.bufferOutOfBounds` if `offset + length > capacity`"*) and the
// bootstrap's pre-check (`2-Parser.cs:13394-13404`, which passes `capacity` down for exactly this). A
// NEGATIVE `offset` or `length` is refused by the same guard: `send`'s `len` is an `int`, so a negative i64
// would arrive as an enormous count and read off the end of the buffer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE BOUND IS `capacity@16` FOR AN OWNED BUFFER AND `length@8` FOR A NON-OWNED ONE — see the
// `pickBound` block below, which carries the measurement.** `__mf_read` states the neighbouring claim (*"a
// non-owned buffer fails it structurally"*) about its own capacity test, and that claim is FALSE on this
// tree for the reason recorded there; it is a defect of that builder, outside this spec's cases, and is
// reported rather than changed here.
function buildManagedSocketSendFrom(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketSendFromName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkoff")
	var checkLength = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschklen")
	var pickBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msbound")
	var viewBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msviewb")
	var ownedBound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msownb")
	var checkCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkcap")
	var outOfBounds = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msoob")
	var doSend = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mssend")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msfail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let offset = 2 as ValueId
	let length = 3 as ValueId
	let socket = emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkOffset, box: box, ids: ids)

	// ⚠⚠ **BOTH OPERANDS ARE TESTED SEPARATELY, AND THE ONE-TEST SHORTCUT IS A MEASURABLY WRONG GUARD.** The
	// tempting form is "`offset + length < 0` catches either negative", and it does not: `offset: 10,
	// length: -5` sums to 5, which is neither negative nor over any capacity ≥ 5 — so the pair sails through
	// and `send` is handed a NEGATIVE length, which its `int` parameter turns into a ~4-billion-byte read off
	// the end of the buffer. The sum is still computed once (the capacity test needs it), but it may not
	// stand in for the two sign tests.
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: checkOffset, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let negativeOffset = emitCmp(module, block: checkOffset, a: offset, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkOffset, op: StdOp.condBranch(negativeOffset, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: checkLength.id))

	let negativeLength = emitCmp(module, block: checkLength, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkLength, op: StdOp.condBranch(negativeLength, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: pickBound.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH FIELD BOUNDS THE READ DEPENDS ON WHO OWNS THE BUFFER, AND `capacity@16` ALONE CANNOT ANSWER
	// IT — MEASURED.** The spec's rule is *"throws `bufferOutOfBounds` if `offset + length > capacity`"*, and
	// that sentence is exact for an OWNED record, where `capacity@16` is a count. For a NON-OWNED one it is
	// not a count at all: a zero-copy view, an `.rdata` byte-string blob, an inline String and an immortal
	// record each stamp a NEGATIVE SENTINEL there (`BufferOwnership`'s four values), and the readable window
	// is `length@8`.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **AND A BARE `extent > capacityExtent` DOES NOT EVEN REFUSE THE SENTINEL, WHICH IS THE TRAP THIS
	// BLOCK EXISTS TO AVOID.** The extent goes through `emitArrayByteExtent` → `emitElementByteLen` →
	// `emitBitsToBytes`, which ends in a LOGICAL shift right — so `capacity = -1` comes back as
	// `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, an enormous POSITIVE bound that every extent is under. MEASURED on this tree:
	// `sendFrom("ping".toByteArray().managed, 2, 9)` — a 9-byte read out of a 4-byte window — was ACCEPTED and
	// sent, and so was every larger one. ⇒ the ownership question must be asked with `emitBufferNotOwned`,
	// the ONE canonical decoder of that field, and never by comparing a byte extent against zero.
	let notOwned = emitBufferNotOwned(module, block: pickBound, record: arrRec, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pickBound, op: StdOp.condBranch(notOwned, thenBlockId: viewBound.id, elseBlockId: ownedBound.id))

	// The bound rides a block arg because it comes from one of two fields — the phi model is the only way a
	// value crosses a merge at this tier.
	let bound = ids.mint()
	checkCap.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(bound, argType: StdType.i64))

	let viewExtent = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: viewBound, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(viewBound, op: StdOp.branch(checkCap.id))
	viewBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkCap.id, argIds: argsOf(viewExtent)))

	let ownedExtent = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: ownedBound, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ownedBound, op: StdOp.branch(checkCap.id))
	ownedBound.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(checkCap.id, argIds: argsOf(ownedExtent)))

	// Both operands are non-negative here, so the sum is a real extent and cannot have wrapped.
	let extent = emitBin(module, block: checkCap, a: offset, b: length, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let overCapacity = emitCmp(module, block: checkCap, a: extent, b: bound, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkCap, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCapacity, thenBlockId: outOfBounds.id, elseBlockId: doSend.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: outOfBounds, flag: ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsFlag, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doSend, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let sendStart = emitBin(module, block: doSend, a: buffer, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let sent = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doSend, operation: GtIoOperation.netSend, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doSend, op: StdOp.osSocketSend(sent, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: sendStart, lenId: length))
	emitSocketTransferOutcome(module, block: doSend, transferred: sent, failed: failed, ok: ok, failureFlag: ManagedSocketSendFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketSendFrom'

// `__ms_recv(box, arrRec) -> (bytesRead, flag)` — receive up to the buffer's byte CAPACITY.
//
// ⭐ **A ZERO IS AN ANSWER, NOT A FAILURE**, per the spec: *"Returns 0 if the peer closed gracefully"*. Only a
// `SOCKET_ERROR` reaches `recvFailed`; `connectionClosed` is the caller's to raise from the 0 (see the
// ordinal block at the top of this file).
//
// ⚠ There is no length PARAMETER, and that is the reference's signature rather than a simplification: the
// method fills `managed` up to its capacity. A caller that wants fewer bytes allocates a smaller buffer,
// which is what `stdlib/TcpClient.maxon:62` does.
function buildManagedSocketRecv(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketRecvName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: managedSocketThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkCap = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mschkcap")
	var doRecv = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrecv")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msfail")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let box = 0 as ValueId
	let arrRec = 1 as ValueId
	let socket = emitLiveSocketOrThrow(module, func: func, entry: entry, join: checkCap, box: box, ids: ids)

	// ⚠⚠ **A NON-OWNED BUFFER IS REFUSED BEFORE THE KERNEL EVER SEES IT, AND THIS IS A WRITE SO THE TEST IS
	// NOT OPTIONAL.** A zero-copy view, an `.rdata` byte-string blob, an inline String and an immortal record
	// all stamp a NEGATIVE SENTINEL in `capacity@16` (`BufferOwnership`'s four values) and none of them may be
	// written in place — one of them is a READ-ONLY image page. Handed on, that sentinel becomes `recv`'s
	// `int len` and the kernel writes ~4 GB through memory somebody else owns.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE TEST AND THE BRANCH DIRECTION ARE `BufferOwnership.emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget`'s, NOT THIS
	// FILE'S.** `__mf_read` is the second surface that fills a caller's buffer from the kernel, and it made
	// the identical mistake this guard exists to refuse — including the `byteExtent`-based form, which does
	// NOT work: `emitArrayByteExtent` ends in a LOGICAL shift right, so a `-1` capacity comes back as
	// `0x1FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, positive and enormous. The measurements for both are recorded at that function.
	//
	// ⚠ It throws `recvFailed` and NOT `bufferOutOfBounds`: the spec gives `recv` exactly one failure variant
	// (*"Throws `recvFailed` on OS error"*) and documents `bufferOutOfBounds` as `sendFrom`'s alone, so the
	// other spelling would be a variant a `match` over this method's contract need not have an arm for. The
	// FLAG is why the shared guard takes a refusal BLOCK rather than choosing one: the two families' enums
	// name nothing in common.
	emitRefuseNonOwnedFillTarget(module, block: checkCap, record: arrRec, refuse: failed, fill: doRecv, ids: ids)

	// The buffer is OWNED here, so `capacity@16` is a real count and its byte extent is non-negative.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doRecv, record: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let byteCapacity = emitArrayByteExtent(module, block: doRecv, arrRec: arrRec, offset: ManagedRecordCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	let received = ids.mint()
	emitIoPark(module, block: doRecv, operation: GtIoOperation.netRecv, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doRecv, op: StdOp.osSocketRecv(received, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: buffer, lenId: byteCapacity))
	emitSocketTransferOutcome(module, block: doRecv, transferred: received, failed: failed, ok: ok, failureFlag: ManagedSocketRecvFailedFlag, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketRecv'

// ⭐ **THE TAIL OF BOTH TRANSFERS — ONE HOME, TWO CALLERS.** `send` and `recv` report failure the same way
// (`SOCKET_ERROR`, i.e. -1) and success as a COUNT, so what follows the op is one shape: test the count
// against the sentinel, and route to this operation's failure variant or to the success exit carrying the
// count. Only the flag differs.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT CAPTURE `GetLastError`, AND THAT IS THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE FILE FAMILY'S
// `emitWin32BoolOutcome`.** Winsock reports through `WSAGetLastError`, not `GetLastError`, and
// `__ManagedSocketError` has no errno-classified variants to route into — the spec gives one catch-all per
// operation. Reading the wrong error API to fill a variant that does not exist would be a wrong answer
// dressed as diagnosis.
//
// `failed` and `ok` arrive from the caller rather than being minted here so the function's block ORDER is
// the caller's to state — it is what the emitted code's layout follows.
function emitSocketTransferOutcome(module StdModule, block IrBlock, transferred ValueId, failed IrBlock, ok IrBlock, failureFlag ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let errorConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SocketError, ids: ids)
	let didFail = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: transferred, b: errorConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(didFail, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitErrorReturn(module, block: failed, flag: failureFlag, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: ok, value: transferred, ids: ids)
end 'emitSocketTransferOutcome'

// `__ms_close(box)` — close the socket, idempotently. Returns nothing and throws nothing, per the spec's
// *"Idempotent. Also called automatically via destructor. Does not throw."*
//
// ⭐ **IDEMPOTENCE IS THE ZEROED HANDLE**, exactly as `__mf_close`'s is: the box is zeroed at allocation and
// this writes the closed sentinel back, so one guard covers "never connected" and "already closed" — which is
// what makes the documented "close then let the destructor close again" sequence safe rather than a second
// `closesocket` on a descriptor the OS may have reused.
function buildManagedSocketClose(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketCloseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var doClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdo")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	let slot = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: box, offset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, ids: ids)
	let closedConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	let alreadyClosed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: slot, b: closedConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	// `entry` dominates `doClose`, so the decoded socket is simply in scope there — the dominance argument
	// `ManagedFileRuntime.emitLiveHandleOrClosedThrow` makes for its own.
	let socket = emitHandleFromSlot(module, block: entry, slot: slot, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyClosed, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: doClose.id))

	emitIoPark(module, block: doClose, operation: GtIoOperation.netClose, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: doClose, value: ManagedSocketClosedHandle, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doClose, record: box, offset: ManagedSocketHandleOffset, value: zero)
	module.setTerminator(doClose, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketClose'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_destruct(box)` — THE RAII DESTRUCTOR, AND THE REASON A `__ManagedSocket` MAY BE DROPPED AT
// ALL.** The refcount-checked teardown a box takes instead of the generic `__mm_decref`: last owner ⇒ close
// the socket, then free the eight bytes; otherwise lower the count and keep the box.
//
// ⭐ **A SOCKET IS NOT MEMORY, WHICH IS WHY THIS EXISTS RATHER THAN A SYNTHESIZED CASCADE** —
// `buildManagedFileDestruct`'s argument, one type over: `_handle` is a scalar `int` field, so
// `structFieldIsManaged` is false and `installStructDestructors` skips the type outright. Answering
// `__mm_decref` would free the eight bytes and leak the socket for the process lifetime, invisibly to the
// exit-101 gate.
//
// ⭐ It reuses `__ms_close` rather than re-emitting the close, so idempotence is stated once: an explicit
// `s.close()` followed by the scope-exit drop finds the sentinel already stored and does nothing.
function buildManagedSocketDestruct(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketDestructName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var drop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drop")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let box = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, func: func, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: box, lastOwner: drop, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(drop, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: ManagedSocketCloseName, args: argsOf(box)))
	emitRawFree(module, block: drop, ptr: box, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(drop, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketDestruct'

// The name this family's ordinals are transcribed under, for `BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s diagnostic — one
// constant so the lookup's message and the claim's message name the same author.
let ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller = b"the __ManagedSocket runtime"

// ⭐ **THE ORDINAL DRIFT GUARD FOR `__ManagedSocketError`** — the twin of `verifyManagedFileOrdinals`, which
// carries the whole argument: the case ORDER is the wire format, the declaration it is verified against is
// `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s, and it is a free function because it needs the `Project` and must run on every
// compile.
export function verifyManagedSocketOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: ManagedSocketErrorTypeName, installer: ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimManagedSocketOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyManagedSocketOrdinals'

function claimManagedSocketOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var socketErrors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: ManagedSocketOrdinalInstaller)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketBufferOutOfBoundsOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketResolveFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketResolveFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketConnectFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketConnectFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketSendFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketSendFailedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketRecvFailedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketRecvFailedOrdinal)
	// A graceful peer close is a plain 0 RETURN from `recv`, so the runtime never throws this — see the
	// ordinal block at the top of this file.
	socketErrors.neverThrown(ManagedSocketConnectionClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketConnectionClosedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.claim(ManagedSocketClosedCaseName, ordinal: ManagedSocketClosedOrdinal)
	socketErrors.requireTotal()
end 'claimManagedSocketOrdinals'

// ⭐ **INSTALL + THE BIDIRECTIONAL DRIFT GUARD**, copied from `installManagedFileRuntime` for its stated
// reason: the predicate above and the `throws` clauses stamped by the builders are ONE fact in two places,
// and a builder that gains or loses a clause without the predicate following is a `try` that silently stops
// being required (or an E3055 on a call that cannot fail). Checked in BOTH directions, and the source-method
// map is asked for every throwing callee in the same traversal (see `GtRuntime.runtimeCalleeSourceMethod`).
export function installManagedSocketRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage, target Target)
	if not usage.usesManagedSocket 'noManagedSocket'
		return
	end 'noManagedSocket'

	buildManagedSocketTcpConnect(module, target: target)
	buildManagedSocketSendFrom(module)
	buildManagedSocketRecv(module)
	buildManagedSocketClose(module)
	buildManagedSocketDestruct(module)

	// ⚠ **THE RESOLVER IS INSTALLED ONLY WHERE ITS SHAPE IS CHOSEN**, exactly as
	// `installCommandLineRuntime` installs one of two argv graphs: a lane with a `getaddrinfo` emits no call
	// to `__ms_resolve4`, and laying it out anyway would be a dozen unreachable functions in every socket
	// program on that lane.
	match socketResolveShape(target) 'shape'
		addrInfoList then break 'shape'
		builtinResolver then installManagedSocketResolver(module, target: target)
	end 'shape'

	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if not isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'notOurs'
			continue
		end 'notOurs'
		let named = isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(func.name)
		let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
		if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
			panic("installManagedSocketRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws` clause but isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'stampedNotNamed'
		if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
			panic("installManagedSocketRuntime: isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted")
		end 'namedNotStamped'

		if named 'hasSourceMethod'
			requireQuotableSourceSpelling(managedSocketSourceMethodName(func.name), callee: func.name)
		end 'hasSourceMethod'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'installManagedSocketRuntime'

// Every callee this file installs — what the drift guard iterates, what `scanRuntimeUsage` reads to set
// `usesManagedSocket`, what `SemanticCheck.calleeNeedsWin32Substrate` refuses on a non-Windows target, and
// what its yield roster reads. A PREFIX test rather than a five-way `or`: every entry point this file
// installs is `__ms_`-named by construction, so a sixth is covered without being remembered here as well as
// declared above.
export function isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ManagedSocketRuntimePrefix)
end 'isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee'

// ==========================================================================
// THE BUILT-IN RESOLVER — the Std graph a lane with no `getaddrinfo` runs
// ==========================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **A HOST NAME BECOMES AN IPv4 ADDRESS IN THREE STEPS, IN THE ORDER EVERY POSIX RESOLVER USES:**
// a NUMERIC address is parsed and answered without touching the network, `/etc/hosts` is consulted next,
// and only then is a DNS query sent to the first `nameserver` in `/etc/resolv.conf`. That order is not a
// preference — `192.0.2.1` and `localhost` must resolve on a machine with no working DNS at all, and
// `specs/managed-socket.md`'s `connect-refused` case is exactly the first of those.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE DNS EXCHANGE IS OVER TCP, WHICH IS WHY THIS NEEDS NO NEW `StdOp`.** DNS is usually spoken over
// UDP, and a datagram socket would need `socket(SOCK_DGRAM)`, `sendto` and `recvfrom` primitives this tier
// does not have. Every resolver MUST also accept TCP (RFC 7766 makes it a requirement, not a fallback), and
// a TCP query is the same message behind a two-byte length prefix — so the five stream ops the socket band
// already owns answer the whole question. MEASURED against the resolver these lanes actually run under:
// a TCP query for a real host answered `rcode 0, ancount 1`, byte-identical in content to the UDP one.
//
// ⚠⚠ **WHAT THIS RESOLVER DOES NOT DO, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED.** It answers IPv4 (`A`) records only —
// which is all `sockaddr_in` can carry and all `__ms_tcp_connect` builds; it reads the FIRST `nameserver`
// line and does not fail over to a second; it does not apply the `search`/`domain` suffixes, so a bare
// unqualified label resolves only through `/etc/hosts`; and it follows no `CNAME` chain of its own — a
// server that answers a CNAME without the A record beside it is a miss. Every one of those is a step
// `getaddrinfo` takes and this does not, and each would be a widening rather than a redesign.

// The answer both the parser and the whole resolver give when there is no address. -1 is not a possible
// `sin_addr` word: it would be 255.255.255.255, the limited broadcast address, which is not a connect
// target — so the sentinel costs no legal answer.
let ResolveNoAddress = -1 as ParsedInt

// The resolver's entry point, plus the eight helpers it is built out of. All are `__ms_`-prefixed, which is
// what puts them inside `isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee` — the drift guard walks them, and no source line can
// name one, so the E3104 roster is unaffected.
let ManagedSocketResolveName = b"__ms_resolve4"
let ManagedSocketIpv4Name = b"__ms_ipv4"
let ManagedSocketHostsName = b"__ms_hosts4"
let ManagedSocketNameserverName = b"__ms_ns4"
let ManagedSocketDnsName = b"__ms_dns4"
let ManagedSocketSlurpName = b"__ms_slurp"
let ManagedSocketBlanksName = b"__ms_blanks"
let ManagedSocketWordName = b"__ms_word"
let ManagedSocketEolName = b"__ms_eol"
let ManagedSocketDnsSkipName = b"__ms_dns_skip"

// The two files a POSIX resolver reads, and nothing chooses them at runtime.
let ResolveHostsPath = b"/etc/hosts"
let ResolveConfPath = b"/etc/resolv.conf"

// The keyword whose line carries a resolver's address. Its LENGTH is compared before its bytes, which is
// what makes the byte loop unreachable for the `search`, `options` and `domain` lines beside it.
let ResolveNameserverKeyword = b"nameserver"

// ⚠ **THE SCRATCH A CONFIGURATION FILE IS READ INTO, AND ITS SIZE IS A STATED LIMIT RATHER THAN A GUESS.**
// One buffer serves both files, one read fills it, and a file longer than this is TRUNCATED — so a host
// defined past 8 KiB of `/etc/hosts` is not found. Both files are conventionally a few hundred bytes; a
// growing read loop would be a second allocator's worth of code for a case neither file reaches.
let ResolveTextBytes = 8192 as ParsedInt

// The scratch a DNS exchange is built and read in. It bounds the QUERY (a name is at most 255 bytes, so a
// query is at most 273) and the RESPONSE, which a server may legally make far larger — an answer section
// precedes the authority and additional sections, so an `A` record is inside this window for every response
// a stub resolver receives in practice, and a longer one is parsed as far as it arrived.
let DnsMessageBytes = 4096 as ParsedInt

// The bytes this file's scanners test. Named because a bare 0x2E in a compare says nothing.
let ResolveDotByte = 0x2E
let ResolveHashByte = 0x23
let ResolveNewlineByte = 0x0A
let ResolveSpaceByte = 0x20
let ResolveTabByte = 0x09
let ResolveDigitZeroByte = 0x30
let ResolveDigitNineByte = 0x39

// ⭐ **A DOTTED QUAD'S FOUR OCTETS, AND WHAT BOUNDS EACH.** The parser accepts exactly four octets of 0..255
// separated by three dots and NOTHING else — no shorthand `a.b`, no octal, no leading `+`. `inet_addr`'s
// historical forms are deliberately absent: they are a source of ambiguity rather than of compatibility, and
// no spec case reaches one.
let Ipv4OctetCount = 4
let Ipv4LastOctetIndex = Ipv4OctetCount - 1
let Ipv4OctetMax = 255
let Ipv4DecimalBase = 10

// `256` as a MULTIPLIER, so each octet lands one byte higher than the last. The address is accumulated in
// NETWORK order — the first octet is the LOW byte — because that is the order `sin_addr` holds it in and
// therefore what `emitSockaddrInWord` places without swapping.
let Ipv4OctetScale = 256

// The DNS facts this file writes and reads. Every one is RFC 1035's and none is a choice.
let DnsPort = 53
let DnsTcpLengthPrefixBytes = 2
let DnsHeaderBytes = 12
let DnsQuestionTailBytes = 4
let DnsTypeA = 1
let DnsClassIn = 1
let DnsRecursionDesiredFlags = 0x0100

// The query id. A stub that opens a FRESH connection per query and reads one response cannot be answered by
// anybody else's query, so the id carries no security here and is a constant; it is echoed back and this
// file does not test it, exactly as it does not test the `QR` bit.
let DnsQueryId = 0x4D58
let DnsQdCountOne = 1
let DnsRcodeMask = 0x0F
let DnsRcodeNoError = 0

// A DNS name ends at a zero LENGTH byte or at a two-byte COMPRESSION POINTER, whose top two bits are set.
let DnsLabelPointerMask = 0xC0
let DnsPointerBytes = 2
let DnsLabelTerminatorBytes = 1

// The longest a single label and a whole name may be. A query is refused rather than truncated past either.
let DnsMaxLabelBytes = 63
let DnsMaxNameBytes = 255

// The fixed fields of a resource record after its name: TYPE, CLASS, TTL and RDLENGTH.
let DnsRecordFixedBytes = 10
let DnsRecordRdLengthOffset = 8
let DnsAddressRdLength = 4

// The offsets inside the message this file writes by hand. Every one is relative to the TCP length prefix,
// which is why they are two higher than RFC 1035's own numbering.
let DnsLengthHighOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let DnsLengthLowOffset = 1 as ByteOffset
let DnsIdHighOffset = 2 as ByteOffset
let DnsIdLowOffset = 3 as ByteOffset
let DnsFlagsHighOffset = 4 as ByteOffset
let DnsQdCountLowOffset = 7 as ByteOffset
let DnsRcodeByteOffset = 5 as ByteOffset
let DnsAnCountHighIndex = 8
let DnsQuestionStart = DnsTcpLengthPrefixBytes + DnsHeaderBytes

// A byte's worth of shift, as a MULTIPLIER — the tier has no narrow store, so a big-endian halfword is
// composed rather than written (`ByteTwoScale`'s argument, one field width down).
let ByteOneScale = 256
let ByteMask = 0xFF

// ⭐ Install the whole resolver. Its shape is chosen once, at `installManagedSocketRuntime`.
function installManagedSocketResolver(module StdModule, target Target)
	buildManagedSocketSpan(module, name: ManagedSocketBlanksName, skipBlanks: true)
	buildManagedSocketSpan(module, name: ManagedSocketWordName, skipBlanks: false)
	buildManagedSocketEol(module)
	buildManagedSocketIpv4(module)
	buildManagedSocketSlurp(module)
	buildManagedSocketHosts(module)
	buildManagedSocketNameserver(module)
	buildManagedSocketDnsSkip(module)
	buildManagedSocketDns(module, target: target)
	buildManagedSocketResolve(module)
end 'installManagedSocketResolver'

// One CONSTANT byte at a CONSTANT offset — the shape the DNS header is written with, where
// `emitStoreByte`'s runtime index would be an add of two constants at every field.
function emitStoreConstByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, offset ByteOffset, value ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let byteValue = emitConst(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(base, offset: offset, valueId: byteValue, valueType: StdType.u8))
end 'emitStoreConstByte'

// Its dual — one byte at a CONSTANT offset, zero-extended.
function emitLoadConstByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, offset ByteOffset, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let loaded = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(loaded, addrId: base, offset: offset, valueType: StdType.u8))
	return loaded
end 'emitLoadConstByte'

// A BIG-ENDIAN halfword at a runtime index — every multi-byte field in a DNS message is one, and this tier
// has no halfword load to do it in one step.
function emitLoadBigEndian16(module StdModule, block IrBlock, buf ValueId, index ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let high = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: buf, index: index, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let lowIndex = emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let low = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: buf, index: lowIndex, ids: ids)
	let scale = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	let placed = emitBin(module, block: block, a: high, b: scale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: placed, b: low, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadBigEndian16'

// The `int` a helper answers, straight out of a call. Every function in this section returns one i64.
function emitResolverCall(module StdModule, block IrBlock, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let result = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(result, callee: callee, args: args))
	return result
end 'emitResolverCall'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE LOOP, TWO INSTALLED FUNCTIONS — `__ms_blanks` SKIPS A RUN OF BLANKS AND `__ms_word` SKIPS A RUN
// OF NON-BLANKS, AND THEY DIFFER ONLY IN WHICH WAY THE PREDICATE BRANCHES.** A blank is a space or a tab; a
// newline is not one, because every caller works inside a line whose end it has already found.
//
// ⚠ **THE PREDICATE HAS TO BRANCH RATHER THAN PRODUCE A VALUE.** This tier has no `setcc`
// (`MmRuntime.emitConstReturn` carries that), so *"is this byte a blank"* is two compares and two edges
// rather than an expression — which is exactly why the two functions share a BUILDER instead of sharing a
// runtime argument.
function buildManagedSocketSpan(module StdModule, name ByteArray, skipBlanks bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msspan")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msspb")
	var tabTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mssptab")
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msspstep")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msspdone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let buf = 0 as ValueId
	let start = 1 as ValueId
	let limit = 2 as ValueId

	let onBlank = step.id if skipBlanks else done.id
	let offBlank = done.id if skipBlanks else step.id

	let pos = ids.mint()
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(start)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: pos, b: limit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let current = emitLoadByte(module, block: body, base: buf, index: pos, ids: ids)
	let space = emitConst(module, block: body, value: ResolveSpaceByte, ids: ids)
	let isSpace = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: current, b: space, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSpace, thenBlockId: onBlank, elseBlockId: tabTest.id))

	let tab = emitConst(module, block: tabTest, value: ResolveTabByte, ids: ids)
	let isTab = emitCmp(module, block: tabTest, a: current, b: tab, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(tabTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(isTab, thenBlockId: onBlank, elseBlockId: offBlank))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: step, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let next = emitBin(module, block: step, a: pos, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	step.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.ret(pos))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketSpan'

// `__ms_eol(buf, pos, end)` — the index of this line's terminating newline, or `end`. Its own function
// rather than a third `buildManagedSocketSpan` specialization because it tests ONE byte and the span tests
// two, and a builder parameterised over the byte SET would be the set written down twice.
function buildManagedSocketEol(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketEolName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mseol")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mseolb")
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mseolstep")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mseoldone")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let buf = 0 as ValueId
	let start = 1 as ValueId
	let limit = 2 as ValueId

	let pos = ids.mint()
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(start)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: pos, b: limit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let current = emitLoadByte(module, block: body, base: buf, index: pos, ids: ids)
	let newline = emitConst(module, block: body, value: ResolveNewlineByte, ids: ids)
	let isNewline = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: current, b: newline, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNewline, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: step.id))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: step, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let next = emitBin(module, block: step, a: pos, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	step.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.ret(pos))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketEol'

// The six values `__ms_ipv4`'s loop carries, in the order its header declares them. A local composer
// because `MmRuntime`'s `argsOf`/`twoArgs`/`threeArgs`/`fourArgs` family stops at four and this loop needs
// six — which is also why the six are named here once rather than pushed at each of the three edges.
function ipv4LoopArgs(pos ValueId, address ValueId, scale ValueId, octet ValueId, digits ValueId, closed ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	var args = fourArgs(pos, second: address, third: scale, fourth: octet)
	args.push(digits)
	args.push(closed)
	return args
end 'ipv4LoopArgs'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_ipv4(buf, len)` — A DOTTED QUAD, OR `ResolveNoAddress`.** Exactly four decimal octets of
// 0..255 separated by three dots, over the byte range `buf[0 .. len)`, and nothing else: no `inet_addr`
// shorthand (`a.b`, `a.b.c`), no octal, no sign, no surrounding blanks. The historical forms are refused
// rather than supported because they are a source of ambiguity — `010` is ten here and eight there — and
// nothing in this compiler's surface produces one.
//
// ⚠ **THE ADDRESS IS ACCUMULATED IN NETWORK ORDER — THE FIRST OCTET IS THE LOW BYTE** — because that is the
// order `sin_addr` holds it in, which is what lets `emitSockaddrInWord` place it without a swap and what
// makes `192.0.2.1` compare equal to `TestNet1NetOrder`.
//
// ⚠ Six values ride the loop: the cursor, the address so far, the next octet's byte SCALE, the octet being
// read, how many digits it has (which is what refuses an EMPTY octet) and how many octets are closed.
function buildManagedSocketIpv4(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketIpv4Name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msip")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipb")
	var digitLow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdlo")
	var digitHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdhi")
	var digit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdig")
	var digitFits = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdfit")
	var dot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdot")
	var dotCount = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdcnt")
	var dotClosed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipdcl")
	var finish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipfin")
	var finishCount = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipfcnt")
	var finishClosed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipfcl")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msipbad")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let buf = 0 as ValueId
	let len = 1 as ValueId

	let pos = ids.mint()
	let address = ids.mint()
	let scale = ids.mint()
	let octet = ids.mint()
	let digits = ids.mint()
	let closed = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(address, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(scale, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(octet, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(digits, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(closed, argType: StdType.i64))

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let firstScale = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: ipv4LoopArgs(zero, address: zero, scale: firstScale, octet: zero, digits: zero, closed: zero)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: pos, b: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: finish.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let current = emitLoadByte(module, block: body, base: buf, index: pos, ids: ids)
	let dotByte = emitConst(module, block: body, value: ResolveDotByte, ids: ids)
	let isDot = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: current, b: dotByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDot, thenBlockId: dot.id, elseBlockId: digitLow.id))

	let zeroDigit = emitConst(module, block: digitLow, value: ResolveDigitZeroByte, ids: ids)
	let belowDigits = emitCmp(module, block: digitLow, a: current, b: zeroDigit, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(digitLow, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowDigits, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: digitHigh.id))

	let nineDigit = emitConst(module, block: digitHigh, value: ResolveDigitNineByte, ids: ids)
	let aboveDigits = emitCmp(module, block: digitHigh, a: current, b: nineDigit, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(digitHigh, op: StdOp.condBranch(aboveDigits, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: digit.id))

	let base = emitConst(module, block: digit, value: Ipv4DecimalBase, ids: ids)
	let shifted = emitBin(module, block: digit, a: octet, b: base, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let digitZero = emitConst(module, block: digit, value: ResolveDigitZeroByte, ids: ids)
	let digitValue = emitBin(module, block: digit, a: current, b: digitZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let grown = emitBin(module, block: digit, a: shifted, b: digitValue, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let octetMax = emitConst(module, block: digit, value: Ipv4OctetMax, ids: ids)
	let overflowed = emitCmp(module, block: digit, a: grown, b: octetMax, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(digit, op: StdOp.condBranch(overflowed, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: digitFits.id))

	let digitStep = emitConst(module, block: digitFits, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let digitsNext = emitBin(module, block: digitFits, a: digits, b: digitStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let posAfterDigit = emitBin(module, block: digitFits, a: pos, b: digitStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(digitFits, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	digitFits.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: ipv4LoopArgs(posAfterDigit, address: address, scale: scale, octet: grown, digits: digitsNext, closed: closed)))

	// A dot closes an octet, and both guards are refusals: an EMPTY octet (`1..2`, a leading dot) and a
	// FOURTH dot. Together they are what holds the accepted grammar to exactly four octets.
	let dotEmptyZero = emitConst(module, block: dot, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let dotEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: dot, a: digits, b: dotEmptyZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dot, op: StdOp.condBranch(dotEmpty, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: dotCount.id))

	let lastIndex = emitConst(module, block: dotCount, value: Ipv4LastOctetIndex, ids: ids)
	let dotsFull = emitCmp(module, block: dotCount, a: closed, b: lastIndex, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dotCount, op: StdOp.condBranch(dotsFull, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: dotClosed.id))

	let dotScale = emitConst(module, block: dotClosed, value: Ipv4OctetScale, ids: ids)
	let placedDot = emitBin(module, block: dotClosed, a: octet, b: scale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let addressDot = emitBin(module, block: dotClosed, a: address, b: placedDot, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let scaleNext = emitBin(module, block: dotClosed, a: scale, b: dotScale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let dotStep = emitConst(module, block: dotClosed, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let closedNext = emitBin(module, block: dotClosed, a: closed, b: dotStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let posAfterDot = emitBin(module, block: dotClosed, a: pos, b: dotStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let dotReset = emitConst(module, block: dotClosed, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dotClosed, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	dotClosed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: ipv4LoopArgs(posAfterDot, address: addressDot, scale: scaleNext, octet: dotReset, digits: dotReset, closed: closedNext)))

	// The end of the range closes the LAST octet. The same empty-octet refusal, and a count that must be
	// EXACTLY three dots — where the dot arm's is a ceiling, because a fourth dot and a fourth octet are not
	// the same event.
	let finishEmptyZero = emitConst(module, block: finish, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let finishEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: finish, a: digits, b: finishEmptyZero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finish, op: StdOp.condBranch(finishEmpty, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: finishCount.id))

	let finishLast = emitConst(module, block: finishCount, value: Ipv4LastOctetIndex, ids: ids)
	let wrongCount = emitCmp(module, block: finishCount, a: closed, b: finishLast, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finishCount, op: StdOp.condBranch(wrongCount, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: finishClosed.id))

	let placedLast = emitBin(module, block: finishClosed, a: octet, b: scale, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let addressLast = emitBin(module, block: finishClosed, a: address, b: placedLast, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finishClosed, op: StdOp.ret(addressLast))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: failed, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketIpv4'

// ⭐ **`__ms_slurp(path, buf, cap)` — A WHOLE CONFIGURATION FILE IN ONE READ, OR 0.** Open, one read, close.
//
// ⚠ **ONE READ, AND THE LIMIT IS STATED RATHER THAN LOOPED AROUND.** `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/resolv.conf`
// are regular files of a few hundred bytes, for which a single `read` returns everything; a file longer
// than `cap` is truncated here, so a definition past that point is not found. A growing read loop would be
// a second allocator's worth of code for a case neither file reaches.
//
// ⚠ A failed open answers 0 rather than throwing: this whole family reports *no address* and never *why*,
// because `__ManagedSocketError` has one resolve variant and a missing `/etc/resolv.conf` is a resolve
// failure like any other.
function buildManagedSocketSlurp(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketSlurpName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var opened = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msslop")
	var read = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msslrd")
	var empty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msslno")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let path = 0 as ValueId
	let buf = 1 as ValueId
	let cap = 2 as ValueId

	let handle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osFileOpenRead(handle, pathPtrId: path))
	let invalid = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: InvalidHandleValue, ids: ids)
	let openFailed = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: handle, b: invalid, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(openFailed, thenBlockId: empty.id, elseBlockId: opened.id))

	let out = emitWin32OutWord(module, block: opened, ids: ids)
	let readOk = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(opened, op: StdOp.osReadFile(readOk, handleId: handle, bufId: buf, lenId: cap, bytesOutPtrId: out))
	module.appendOp(opened, op: StdOp.osFileClose(handle))
	let count = emitLoadField(module, block: opened, record: out, offset: Win32OutWordAnswerOffset, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: opened, ptr: out, ids: ids)
	let readFalse = emitConst(module, block: opened, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let readFailed = emitCmp(module, block: opened, a: readOk, b: readFalse, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(opened, op: StdOp.condBranch(readFailed, thenBlockId: empty.id, elseBlockId: read.id))

	module.setTerminator(read, op: StdOp.ret(count))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: empty, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketSlurp'

// ⭐ **A COMPILE-TIME cstring IN A BLOCK THIS FUNCTION OWNS.** `MmRuntime.emitLiteralCString` puts the same
// bytes in a `__slab_alloc` block and none of its callers releases one, which is sound there because each
// runs ONCE PER PROCESS (a `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` read, a `PATH` lookup). A resolve runs once per CONNECT, so
// its scratch has to be released — and therefore has to come from the tracked allocator `emitRawFree`
// answers. The terminator needs no store: an `__mm_alloc` block is zeroed.
function emitOwnedCString(module StdModule, block IrBlock, text ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let size = emitConst(module, block: block, value: text.count() + 1, ids: ids)
	let buf = emitRawAlloc(module, block: block, byteCount: size, ids: ids)

	for k in 0 upto text.count() 'eachByte'
		let ch = try text.get(k) otherwise panic("emitOwnedCString: text.get out of range at {k} of {text.count()}")
		emitStoreConstByte(module, block: block, base: buf, offset: k as ByteOffset, value: ch as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	end 'eachByte'

	return buf
end 'emitOwnedCString'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LINE WALK BOTH CONFIGURATION-FILE SCANNERS ARE BUILT ON — read the file, then step over it one
// line at a time.** `/etc/hosts` and `/etc/resolv.conf` ask different questions OF a line and the same
// question ABOUT the file, so the skeleton is written once: the path cstring and its release, the read, the
// end-of-line for each step, and the advance past the newline.
//
// ⚠ **THE CALLER MINTS `linePos` AND `lineEnd` AND OWNS EVERY BLOCK**, which is `emitCStringLength`'s
// arrangement and for its reason: a builder answering with both a block and two values would have to return
// a record, and a record here is a heap box per call site. The caller carries on inside `lineStart` — where
// `lineEnd` is already in scope — and terminates that block itself.
function emitResolverLineWalk(module StdModule, entry IrBlock, lineHeader IrBlock, lineStart IrBlock, nextLine IrBlock, notFound IrBlock, path ByteArray, buf ValueId, cap ValueId, linePos ValueId, lineEnd ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let pathBuf = emitOwnedCString(module, block: entry, text: path, ids: ids)
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(count, callee: ManagedSocketSlurpName, args: threeArgs(pathBuf, second: buf, third: cap)))
	emitRawFree(module, block: entry, ptr: pathBuf, ids: ids)
	let firstLine = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(lineHeader.id))
	lineHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(linePos, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(lineHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(firstLine)))

	let linesDone = emitCmp(module, block: lineHeader, a: linePos, b: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lineHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(linesDone, thenBlockId: notFound.id, elseBlockId: lineStart.id))

	module.appendOp(lineStart, op: StdOp.call(lineEnd, callee: ManagedSocketEolName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: linePos, third: count)))

	// The newline itself is stepped over; at the end of the buffer `lineEnd` IS the count, so the next
	// header test ends the walk.
	let lineStep = emitConst(module, block: nextLine, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextLineStart = emitBin(module, block: nextLine, a: lineEnd, b: lineStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nextLine, op: StdOp.branch(lineHeader.id))
	nextLine.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(lineHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(nextLineStart)))
end 'emitResolverLineWalk'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_hosts4(host, hostLen, buf, cap)` — `/etc/hosts`, THE FIRST PLACE A NAME IS LOOKED UP AFTER A
// NUMERIC PARSE FAILS.** A line is `address name [name…]`, a `#` begins a comment wherever it appears, and
// the answer is the line's FIRST field parsed as a dotted quad. `localhost` resolving with no network at all
// is what this step exists for.
//
// ⚠ The scratch buffer is the CALLER's, because `__ms_ns4` reads a second file into the same one and a
// resolve should allocate it once.
//
// ⚠ **THE COMPARISON IS LENGTH-FIRST**, which is what keeps the byte loop off every unrelated name in the
// file rather than only off the ones that differ early.
function buildManagedSocketHosts(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketHostsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lineHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshl")
	var lineStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshls")
	var haveAddress = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshaddr")
	var namesStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshns")
	var nameHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshnh")
	var nameCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshnc")
	var lengthCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshlen")
	var compare = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshcmp")
	var matched = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshhit")
	var nextName = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshnn")
	var nextLine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshnl")
	var notFound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mshno")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let host = 0 as ValueId
	let hostLen = 1 as ValueId
	let buf = 2 as ValueId
	let cap = 3 as ValueId

	let linePos = ids.mint()
	let lineEnd = ids.mint()
	emitResolverLineWalk(module, entry: entry, lineHeader: lineHeader, lineStart: lineStart, nextLine: nextLine, notFound: notFound, path: ResolveHostsPath, buf: buf, cap: cap, linePos: linePos, lineEnd: lineEnd, ids: ids)

	let addressStart = emitResolverCall(module, block: lineStart, callee: ManagedSocketBlanksName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: linePos, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let addressEnd = emitResolverCall(module, block: lineStart, callee: ManagedSocketWordName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: addressStart, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let addressEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: lineStart, a: addressEnd, b: addressStart, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lineStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(addressEmpty, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: haveAddress.id))

	let addressFirst = emitLoadByte(module, block: haveAddress, base: buf, index: addressStart, ids: ids)
	let hashByte = emitConst(module, block: haveAddress, value: ResolveHashByte, ids: ids)
	let addressIsComment = emitCmp(module, block: haveAddress, a: addressFirst, b: hashByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveAddress, op: StdOp.condBranch(addressIsComment, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: namesStart.id))

	// The name walk's cursor rides a block arg, so the entry into it is a plain branch — a `condBranch`
	// carries no edge arguments at this tier.
	let nameCursor = ids.mint()
	module.setTerminator(namesStart, op: StdOp.branch(nameHeader.id))
	nameHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(nameCursor, argType: StdType.i64))
	namesStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nameHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(addressEnd)))

	let nameStart = emitResolverCall(module, block: nameHeader, callee: ManagedSocketBlanksName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: nameCursor, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let nameEnd = emitResolverCall(module, block: nameHeader, callee: ManagedSocketWordName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: nameStart, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let namesDone = emitCmp(module, block: nameHeader, a: nameEnd, b: nameStart, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(namesDone, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: nameCheck.id))

	let nameFirst = emitLoadByte(module, block: nameCheck, base: buf, index: nameStart, ids: ids)
	let nameHash = emitConst(module, block: nameCheck, value: ResolveHashByte, ids: ids)
	let nameIsComment = emitCmp(module, block: nameCheck, a: nameFirst, b: nameHash, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(nameIsComment, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: lengthCheck.id))

	let nameLength = emitBin(module, block: lengthCheck, a: nameEnd, b: nameStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let sameLength = emitCmp(module, block: lengthCheck, a: nameLength, b: hostLen, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lengthCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(sameLength, thenBlockId: compare.id, elseBlockId: nextName.id))

	let namePtr = emitBin(module, block: compare, a: buf, b: nameStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitByteRangeCompareLoop(module, func: func, cur: compare, aBuf: namePtr, bBuf: host, len: hostLen, allMatch: matched, notEqual: nextName, ids: ids)

	let addressPtr = emitBin(module, block: matched, a: buf, b: addressStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let addressLength = emitBin(module, block: matched, a: addressEnd, b: addressStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let hostsAddress = emitResolverCall(module, block: matched, callee: ManagedSocketIpv4Name, args: twoArgs(addressPtr, second: addressLength), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(matched, op: StdOp.ret(hostsAddress))

	module.setTerminator(nextName, op: StdOp.branch(nameHeader.id))
	nextName.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nameHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(nameEnd)))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: notFound, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketHosts'

// ⭐ **`__ms_ns4(buf, cap)` — THE FIRST `nameserver` IN `/etc/resolv.conf`, OR `ResolveNoAddress`.** The
// same line walk as `/etc/hosts` with a different question of each line: the first field must be the
// keyword, and the second must parse as a dotted quad.
//
// ⚠ **THE FIRST ONE ONLY.** A `resolv.conf` may list several and `getaddrinfo` fails over between them on a
// timeout; this resolver has no timeout to fail over on (see `buildManagedSocketDns`), so a second entry
// would be reached only when the first REFUSES a connection, and answering *no address* is the same outcome
// one round trip sooner.
function buildManagedSocketNameserver(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketNameserverName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lineHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnl")
	var lineStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnls")
	var keyCompare = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnkc")
	var keyMatched = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnkm")
	var haveValue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnv")
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnhit")
	var nextLine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnnl")
	var notFound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msnno")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let buf = 0 as ValueId
	let cap = 1 as ValueId

	let keyword = emitOwnedCString(module, block: entry, text: ResolveNameserverKeyword, ids: ids)
	let keywordLength = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: ResolveNameserverKeyword.count(), ids: ids)
	let linePos = ids.mint()
	let lineEnd = ids.mint()
	emitResolverLineWalk(module, entry: entry, lineHeader: lineHeader, lineStart: lineStart, nextLine: nextLine, notFound: notFound, path: ResolveConfPath, buf: buf, cap: cap, linePos: linePos, lineEnd: lineEnd, ids: ids)

	let keyStart = emitResolverCall(module, block: lineStart, callee: ManagedSocketBlanksName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: linePos, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let keyEnd = emitResolverCall(module, block: lineStart, callee: ManagedSocketWordName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: keyStart, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let keyLength = emitBin(module, block: lineStart, a: keyEnd, b: keyStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let keySized = emitCmp(module, block: lineStart, a: keyLength, b: keywordLength, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lineStart, op: StdOp.condBranch(keySized, thenBlockId: keyCompare.id, elseBlockId: nextLine.id))

	let keyPtr = emitBin(module, block: keyCompare, a: buf, b: keyStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitByteRangeCompareLoop(module, func: func, cur: keyCompare, aBuf: keyPtr, bBuf: keyword, len: keywordLength, allMatch: keyMatched, notEqual: nextLine, ids: ids)

	let valueStart = emitResolverCall(module, block: keyMatched, callee: ManagedSocketBlanksName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: keyEnd, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let valueEnd = emitResolverCall(module, block: keyMatched, callee: ManagedSocketWordName, args: threeArgs(buf, second: valueStart, third: lineEnd), ids: ids)
	let valueEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: keyMatched, a: valueEnd, b: valueStart, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(keyMatched, op: StdOp.condBranch(valueEmpty, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: haveValue.id))

	let valuePtr = emitBin(module, block: haveValue, a: buf, b: valueStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let valueLength = emitBin(module, block: haveValue, a: valueEnd, b: valueStart, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let serverAddress = emitResolverCall(module, block: haveValue, callee: ManagedSocketIpv4Name, args: twoArgs(valuePtr, second: valueLength), ids: ids)
	let noAddress = emitConst(module, block: haveValue, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)
	let unparsed = emitCmp(module, block: haveValue, a: serverAddress, b: noAddress, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveValue, op: StdOp.condBranch(unparsed, thenBlockId: nextLine.id, elseBlockId: found.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: found, ptr: keyword, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.ret(serverAddress))

	emitRawFree(module, block: notFound, ptr: keyword, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: notFound, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketNameserver'

// ⭐ **`__ms_dns_skip(buf, pos, end)` — PAST ONE DNS NAME, WHEREVER IT ENDS.** A name is a run of
// length-prefixed labels closed by a zero byte, OR a two-byte COMPRESSION POINTER whose top two bits are
// set — and an answer's name is nearly always the pointer form, because it points back at the question.
// Neither form is FOLLOWED here: this walk only needs the byte AFTER the name.
function buildManagedSocketDnsSkip(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketDnsSkipName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdn")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnb")
	var root = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnr")
	var pointerTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnpt")
	var pointer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnp")
	var label = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnl")
	var truncated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdnt")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let buf = 0 as ValueId
	let start = 1 as ValueId
	let limit = 2 as ValueId

	let pos = ids.mint()
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pos, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(start)))

	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: pos, b: limit, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: truncated.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let current = emitLoadByte(module, block: body, base: buf, index: pos, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isRoot = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: current, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(isRoot, thenBlockId: root.id, elseBlockId: pointerTest.id))

	let terminatorBytes = emitConst(module, block: root, value: DnsLabelTerminatorBytes, ids: ids)
	let afterRoot = emitBin(module, block: root, a: pos, b: terminatorBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(root, op: StdOp.ret(afterRoot))

	let pointerMask = emitConst(module, block: pointerTest, value: DnsLabelPointerMask, ids: ids)
	let masked = emitBin(module, block: pointerTest, a: current, b: pointerMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let isPointer = emitCmp(module, block: pointerTest, a: masked, b: pointerMask, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pointerTest, op: StdOp.condBranch(isPointer, thenBlockId: pointer.id, elseBlockId: label.id))

	let pointerBytes = emitConst(module, block: pointer, value: DnsPointerBytes, ids: ids)
	let afterPointer = emitBin(module, block: pointer, a: pos, b: pointerBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pointer, op: StdOp.ret(afterPointer))

	let labelPrefix = emitConst(module, block: label, value: DnsLabelTerminatorBytes, ids: ids)
	let afterPrefix = emitBin(module, block: label, a: pos, b: labelPrefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let afterLabel = emitBin(module, block: label, a: afterPrefix, b: current, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(label, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	label.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(afterLabel)))

	// A name that runs off the end of what arrived answers the end itself, which every caller then fails its
	// own bounds check against — one refusal, at the site that knows what it was about to read.
	module.setTerminator(truncated, op: StdOp.ret(pos))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketDnsSkip'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__ms_dns4(host, hostLen, server)` — ONE `A` QUERY OVER TCP, AND THE FIRST ADDRESS IT ANSWERS.**
//
// The exchange is RFC 1035's, over the stream transport RFC 7766 makes mandatory: a two-byte length prefix,
// a twelve-byte header asking for RECURSION, one question of type `A` class `IN`, and a response whose
// answer section is walked for the first four-byte `A` record. The query is built in one block, the socket
// phase is five ops, and the parse is a bounded walk — nothing here is retried and nothing is cached.
//
// ⭐ **TCP RATHER THAN UDP IS WHAT MAKES THIS COST NO NEW `StdOp`.** A datagram query would need
// `socket(SOCK_DGRAM)`, `sendto` and `recvfrom` primitives the Std tier does not define; the five STREAM ops
// the socket band already owns answer the whole question, and every resolver must accept TCP.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THERE IS NO TIMEOUT, AND THAT IS THE ONE PROPERTY A CALLER CAN FEEL.** A resolver that accepts the
// connection and never answers holds the green thread until the kernel's own TCP timeout. Bounding it needs
// `setsockopt(SO_RCVTIMEO)` or a non-blocking connect — a primitive this tier does not have — so it is
// recorded here rather than half-solved: the failure mode is a SLOW resolve, never a wrong address.
//
// ⚠ The query id is echoed and NOT checked: a fresh connection carries exactly one exchange, so there is no
// other answer it could be confused with (see `DnsQueryId`).
function buildManagedSocketDns(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketDnsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var sized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdsz")
	var nameHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdq")
	var nameByte = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqb")
	var nameChar = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqc")
	var nameDot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqd")
	var nameDotSized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqds")
	var nameFinish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqf")
	var nameFinishSized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqfs")
	var question = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqq")
	var dial = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msddial")
	var connectStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdconn")
	var sendStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdsend")
	var recvStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrs")
	var recvHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrh")
	var recvFull = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrf")
	var recvRead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrr")
	var recvEof = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdre")
	var recvGot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrg")
	var recvCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrc")
	var recvDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrd")
	var recvMore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrm")
	var parse = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdp")
	var rcodeCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrco")
	var answerCount = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdac")
	var skipQuestion = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdsq")
	var answerHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdah")
	var answerBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdab")
	var answerFields = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdaf")
	var addressRecord = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdar")
	var addressRoom = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdaroom")
	var haveAddress = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdhit")
	var answerNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdan")
	var closeFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdclf")
	var releaseFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdrel")
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdno")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let host = 0 as ValueId
	let hostLen = 1 as ValueId
	let server = 2 as ValueId

	// A name longer than a DNS name may be, and the empty name, are refused before anything is allocated.
	let nameMax = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: DnsMaxNameBytes, ids: ids)
	let nameTooLong = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: hostLen, b: nameMax, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(nameTooLong, thenBlockId: failed.id, elseBlockId: sized.id))

	// The header. `ancount`, `nscount` and `arcount` are left as the zeroes `__mm_alloc` produced.
	let message = emitRawAlloc(module, block: sized, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: sized, value: DnsMessageBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitStoreConstByte(module, block: sized, base: message, offset: DnsIdHighOffset, value: DnsQueryId / ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreConstByte(module, block: sized, base: message, offset: DnsIdLowOffset, value: DnsQueryId mod ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreConstByte(module, block: sized, base: message, offset: DnsFlagsHighOffset, value: DnsRecursionDesiredFlags / ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreConstByte(module, block: sized, base: message, offset: DnsQdCountLowOffset, value: DnsQdCountOne, ids: ids)

	// The QNAME walk carries three values: how far into the host name it has read, where the next byte is
	// written, and where THIS label's length byte is waiting to be filled in.
	let hostIndex = ids.mint()
	let writeAt = ids.mint()
	let lengthAt = ids.mint()
	nameHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(hostIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	nameHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(writeAt, argType: StdType.i64))
	nameHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(lengthAt, argType: StdType.i64))
	let hostFirst = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let firstLengthAt = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: DnsQuestionStart, ids: ids)
	let firstWriteAt = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: DnsQuestionStart + DnsLabelTerminatorBytes, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sized, op: StdOp.branch(nameHeader.id))
	sized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nameHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(hostFirst, second: firstWriteAt, third: firstLengthAt)))

	let nameDone = emitCmp(module, block: nameHeader, a: hostIndex, b: hostLen, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(nameDone, thenBlockId: nameFinish.id, elseBlockId: nameByte.id))

	let hostByte = emitLoadByte(module, block: nameByte, base: host, index: hostIndex, ids: ids)
	let dotByte = emitConst(module, block: nameByte, value: ResolveDotByte, ids: ids)
	let atDot = emitCmp(module, block: nameByte, a: hostByte, b: dotByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameByte, op: StdOp.condBranch(atDot, thenBlockId: nameDot.id, elseBlockId: nameChar.id))

	emitStoreByte(module, block: nameChar, base: message, index: writeAt, value: hostByte, ids: ids)
	let charStep = emitConst(module, block: nameChar, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let hostNext = emitBin(module, block: nameChar, a: hostIndex, b: charStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let writeNext = emitBin(module, block: nameChar, a: writeAt, b: charStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameChar, op: StdOp.branch(nameHeader.id))
	nameChar.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nameHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(hostNext, second: writeNext, third: lengthAt)))

	// A dot closes a label. An EMPTY label — a leading dot, a doubled dot, a trailing dot — is a malformed
	// name rather than a query to send, and so is one over 63 bytes.
	let dotLabelLength = emitDnsLabelLength(module, block: nameDot, writeAt: writeAt, lengthAt: lengthAt, ids: ids)
	emitDnsLabelGuard(module, func: func, block: nameDot, labelLength: dotLabelLength, bad: releaseFailed, ok: nameDotSized, ids: ids)

	emitStoreByte(module, block: nameDotSized, base: message, index: lengthAt, value: dotLabelLength, ids: ids)
	let dotStep = emitConst(module, block: nameDotSized, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let hostAfterDot = emitBin(module, block: nameDotSized, a: hostIndex, b: dotStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let writeAfterDot = emitBin(module, block: nameDotSized, a: writeAt, b: dotStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameDotSized, op: StdOp.branch(nameHeader.id))
	nameDotSized.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(nameHeader.id, argIds: threeArgs(hostAfterDot, second: writeAfterDot, third: writeAt)))

	let finalLabelLength = emitDnsLabelLength(module, block: nameFinish, writeAt: writeAt, lengthAt: lengthAt, ids: ids)
	emitDnsLabelGuard(module, func: func, block: nameFinish, labelLength: finalLabelLength, bad: releaseFailed, ok: nameFinishSized, ids: ids)

	// The last label's length, the ROOT label that closes the name, then QTYPE and QCLASS. The message ends
	// four bytes past the root, and the TCP length prefix counts everything after itself.
	emitStoreByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: lengthAt, value: finalLabelLength, ids: ids)
	emitStoreOffsetByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: writeAt, delta: 0, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreOffsetByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: writeAt, delta: 1, value: DnsTypeA / ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreOffsetByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: writeAt, delta: 2, value: DnsTypeA mod ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreOffsetByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: writeAt, delta: 3, value: DnsClassIn / ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	emitStoreOffsetByte(module, block: nameFinishSized, base: message, index: writeAt, delta: 4, value: DnsClassIn mod ByteOneScale, ids: ids)
	let messageTail = emitConst(module, block: nameFinishSized, value: DnsLabelTerminatorBytes + DnsQuestionTailBytes, ids: ids)
	let sendLength = emitBin(module, block: nameFinishSized, a: writeAt, b: messageTail, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nameFinishSized, op: StdOp.branch(question.id))

	let prefixBytes = emitConst(module, block: question, value: DnsTcpLengthPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let declaredLength = emitBin(module, block: question, a: sendLength, b: prefixBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let lengthShift = emitConst(module, block: question, value: BitsPerByte, ids: ids)
	let lengthHigh = emitBin(module, block: question, a: declaredLength, b: lengthShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let lengthMask = emitConst(module, block: question, value: ByteMask, ids: ids)
	let lengthLow = emitBin(module, block: question, a: declaredLength, b: lengthMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(question, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(message, offset: DnsLengthHighOffset, valueId: lengthHigh, valueType: StdType.u8))
	module.appendOp(question, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(message, offset: DnsLengthLowOffset, valueId: lengthLow, valueType: StdType.u8))
	module.setTerminator(question, op: StdOp.branch(dial.id))

	let socket = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(dial, op: StdOp.osSocketCreate(socket))
	let invalidSocket = emitConst(module, block: dial, value: InvalidSocket, ids: ids)
	let socketFailed = emitCmp(module, block: dial, a: socket, b: invalidSocket, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dial, op: StdOp.condBranch(socketFailed, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: connectStep.id))

	let address = emitRawAlloc(module, block: connectStep, byteCount: emitConst(module, block: connectStep, value: SockaddrInBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let dnsPort = emitConst(module, block: connectStep, value: DnsPort, ids: ids)
	let addressWord = emitSockaddrInWord(module, block: connectStep, port: dnsPort, ip: server, familyHalfword: sockaddrFamilyHalfword(target), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: connectStep, record: address, offset: SockaddrFamilyPortAddrOffset, value: addressWord)
	let dialResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(connectStep, op: StdOp.osSocketConnect(dialResult, sockId: socket, addrPtrId: address))
	emitRawFree(module, block: connectStep, ptr: address, ids: ids)
	let dialOk = emitConst(module, block: connectStep, value: WinsockCallOk, ids: ids)
	let dialFailed = emitCmp(module, block: connectStep, a: dialResult, b: dialOk, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(connectStep, op: StdOp.condBranch(dialFailed, thenBlockId: closeFailed.id, elseBlockId: sendStep.id))

	// A short send is treated as a failure rather than looped over: the query is at most 273 bytes and a
	// stream socket with an empty send buffer takes it whole, so a partial write here is a broken connection
	// wearing a byte count.
	let sent = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(sendStep, op: StdOp.osSocketSend(sent, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: message, lenId: sendLength))
	let sendShort = emitCmp(module, block: sendStep, a: sent, b: sendLength, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sendStep, op: StdOp.condBranch(sendShort, thenBlockId: closeFailed.id, elseBlockId: recvStart.id))

	// ⚠ **THE READ LOOPS BECAUSE TCP IS A STREAM.** A response arrives in as many pieces as the network
	// chooses, so the loop reads until the declared length has arrived, the peer closes, the scratch is full,
	// or the read fails — and the parse below is bounded by what actually arrived either way.
	let received = ids.mint()
	recvHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(received, argType: StdType.i64))
	let receivedFirst = emitConst(module, block: recvStart, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recvStart, op: StdOp.branch(recvHeader.id))
	recvStart.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(recvHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(receivedFirst)))

	let capacity = emitConst(module, block: recvHeader, value: DnsMessageBytes, ids: ids)
	let room = emitBin(module, block: recvHeader, a: capacity, b: received, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let empty = emitConst(module, block: recvHeader, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noRoom = emitCmp(module, block: recvHeader, a: room, b: empty, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recvHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(noRoom, thenBlockId: recvFull.id, elseBlockId: recvRead.id))

	module.setTerminator(recvFull, op: StdOp.branch(parse.id))
	recvFull.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parse.id, argIds: argsOf(received)))

	let readAt = emitBin(module, block: recvRead, a: message, b: received, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let readCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recvRead, op: StdOp.osSocketRecv(readCount, sockId: socket, bufPtrId: readAt, lenId: room))
	let readZero = emitConst(module, block: recvRead, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let readEnded = emitCmp(module, block: recvRead, a: readCount, b: readZero, pred: StdCmpPred.le, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recvRead, op: StdOp.condBranch(readEnded, thenBlockId: recvEof.id, elseBlockId: recvGot.id))

	module.setTerminator(recvEof, op: StdOp.branch(parse.id))
	recvEof.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parse.id, argIds: argsOf(received)))

	let receivedNext = emitBin(module, block: recvGot, a: received, b: readCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let prefixSize = emitConst(module, block: recvGot, value: DnsTcpLengthPrefixBytes, ids: ids)
	let prefixMissing = emitCmp(module, block: recvGot, a: receivedNext, b: prefixSize, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recvGot, op: StdOp.condBranch(prefixMissing, thenBlockId: recvMore.id, elseBlockId: recvCheck.id))

	let lengthIndex = emitConst(module, block: recvCheck, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let declared = emitLoadBigEndian16(module, block: recvCheck, buf: message, index: lengthIndex, ids: ids)
	let wanted = emitBin(module, block: recvCheck, a: declared, b: prefixSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let complete = emitCmp(module, block: recvCheck, a: receivedNext, b: wanted, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recvCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(complete, thenBlockId: recvDone.id, elseBlockId: recvMore.id))

	module.setTerminator(recvDone, op: StdOp.branch(parse.id))
	recvDone.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(parse.id, argIds: argsOf(receivedNext)))

	module.setTerminator(recvMore, op: StdOp.branch(recvHeader.id))
	recvMore.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(recvHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(receivedNext)))

	// The socket has answered everything it is going to; the parse reads only the scratch.
	let arrived = ids.mint()
	parse.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(arrived, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(parse, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	let minimumBytes = emitConst(module, block: parse, value: DnsQuestionStart, ids: ids)
	let tooShort = emitCmp(module, block: parse, a: arrived, b: minimumBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parse, op: StdOp.condBranch(tooShort, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: rcodeCheck.id))

	let rcodeByte = emitLoadConstByte(module, block: rcodeCheck, base: message, offset: DnsRcodeByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let rcodeMask = emitConst(module, block: rcodeCheck, value: DnsRcodeMask, ids: ids)
	let rcode = emitBin(module, block: rcodeCheck, a: rcodeByte, b: rcodeMask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	let noError = emitConst(module, block: rcodeCheck, value: DnsRcodeNoError, ids: ids)
	let serverRefused = emitCmp(module, block: rcodeCheck, a: rcode, b: noError, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(rcodeCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(serverRefused, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: answerCount.id))

	let answerCountIndex = emitConst(module, block: answerCount, value: DnsAnCountHighIndex, ids: ids)
	let answers = emitLoadBigEndian16(module, block: answerCount, buf: message, index: answerCountIndex, ids: ids)
	let noAnswers = emitConst(module, block: answerCount, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyAnswer = emitCmp(module, block: answerCount, a: answers, b: noAnswers, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(answerCount, op: StdOp.condBranch(emptyAnswer, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: skipQuestion.id))

	let questionStart = emitConst(module, block: skipQuestion, value: DnsQuestionStart, ids: ids)
	let afterQuestionName = emitResolverCall(module, block: skipQuestion, callee: ManagedSocketDnsSkipName, args: threeArgs(message, second: questionStart, third: arrived), ids: ids)
	let questionTail = emitConst(module, block: skipQuestion, value: DnsQuestionTailBytes, ids: ids)
	let firstAnswer = emitBin(module, block: skipQuestion, a: afterQuestionName, b: questionTail, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let firstIndex = emitConst(module, block: skipQuestion, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(skipQuestion, op: StdOp.branch(answerHeader.id))
	let answerAt = ids.mint()
	let answerIndex = ids.mint()
	answerHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(answerAt, argType: StdType.i64))
	answerHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(answerIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	skipQuestion.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answerHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(firstAnswer, second: firstIndex)))

	let answersDone = emitCmp(module, block: answerHeader, a: answerIndex, b: answers, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(answerHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(answersDone, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: answerBody.id))

	let afterAnswerName = emitResolverCall(module, block: answerBody, callee: ManagedSocketDnsSkipName, args: threeArgs(message, second: answerAt, third: arrived), ids: ids)
	let fixedBytes = emitConst(module, block: answerBody, value: DnsRecordFixedBytes, ids: ids)
	let recordData = emitBin(module, block: answerBody, a: afterAnswerName, b: fixedBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let recordOverruns = emitCmp(module, block: answerBody, a: recordData, b: arrived, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(answerBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(recordOverruns, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: answerFields.id))

	let recordType = emitLoadBigEndian16(module, block: answerFields, buf: message, index: afterAnswerName, ids: ids)
	let rdLengthOffset = emitConst(module, block: answerFields, value: DnsRecordRdLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let rdLengthIndex = emitBin(module, block: answerFields, a: afterAnswerName, b: rdLengthOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let rdLength = emitLoadBigEndian16(module, block: answerFields, buf: message, index: rdLengthIndex, ids: ids)
	let typeA = emitConst(module, block: answerFields, value: DnsTypeA, ids: ids)
	let notAddress = emitCmp(module, block: answerFields, a: recordType, b: typeA, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(answerFields, op: StdOp.condBranch(notAddress, thenBlockId: answerNext.id, elseBlockId: addressRecord.id))

	let addressBytes = emitConst(module, block: addressRecord, value: DnsAddressRdLength, ids: ids)
	let wrongWidth = emitCmp(module, block: addressRecord, a: rdLength, b: addressBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(addressRecord, op: StdOp.condBranch(wrongWidth, thenBlockId: answerNext.id, elseBlockId: addressRoom.id))

	let addressEnd = emitBin(module, block: addressRoom, a: recordData, b: addressBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let addressOverruns = emitCmp(module, block: addressRoom, a: addressEnd, b: arrived, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(addressRoom, op: StdOp.condBranch(addressOverruns, thenBlockId: releaseFailed.id, elseBlockId: haveAddress.id))

	// ⚠ **THE FOUR RDATA BYTES ARE COMPOSED LOW-BYTE-FIRST**, which is not a byte swap: they arrive in
	// NETWORK order and `sin_addr` holds them in network order, so the word this builds is the one
	// `emitSockaddrInWord` places unchanged — and the one `TestNet1NetOrder` is written to match.
	let resolved = emitDnsAddressWord(module, block: haveAddress, message: message, at: recordData, ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: haveAddress, ptr: message, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveAddress, op: StdOp.ret(resolved))

	let recordNext = emitBin(module, block: answerNext, a: recordData, b: rdLength, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let indexStep = emitConst(module, block: answerNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let indexNext = emitBin(module, block: answerNext, a: answerIndex, b: indexStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(answerNext, op: StdOp.branch(answerHeader.id))
	answerNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answerHeader.id, argIds: twoArgs(recordNext, second: indexNext)))

	module.appendOp(closeFailed, op: StdOp.osSocketClose(socket))
	module.setTerminator(closeFailed, op: StdOp.branch(releaseFailed.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: releaseFailed, ptr: message, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: releaseFailed, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	emitConstReturn(module, block: failed, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketDns'

// How many bytes the label that started at `lengthAt` holds — the write cursor, less that length byte and
// the byte it occupies. Both label boundaries compute it and neither may compute it differently.
function emitDnsLabelLength(module StdModule, block IrBlock, writeAt ValueId, lengthAt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let span = emitBin(module, block: block, a: writeAt, b: lengthAt, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let prefix = emitConst(module, block: block, value: DnsLabelTerminatorBytes, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: span, b: prefix, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
end 'emitDnsLabelLength'

// ⭐ **AN EMPTY LABEL AND AN OVERLONG ONE ARE BOTH REFUSALS, AND THEY ARE TWO EDGES RATHER THAN ONE
// EXPRESSION.** A leading dot, a doubled dot and a trailing dot all produce the empty case, and every one
// of them is a malformed name rather than a query worth sending. The two tests cannot be OR-ed into a value
// because this tier has no `setcc` (`MmRuntime.emitConstReturn` carries that), so a compound condition is
// blocks — which is why both boundaries call this rather than each spelling the pair.
function emitDnsLabelGuard(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, labelLength ValueId, bad IrBlock, ok IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var upper = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msdqlim")

	let shortest = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let belowOne = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: labelLength, b: shortest, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(belowOne, thenBlockId: bad.id, elseBlockId: upper.id))

	let longest = emitConst(module, block: upper, value: DnsMaxLabelBytes, ids: ids)
	let aboveLimit = emitCmp(module, block: upper, a: labelLength, b: longest, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(upper, op: StdOp.condBranch(aboveLimit, thenBlockId: bad.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))
end 'emitDnsLabelGuard'

// One CONSTANT byte at a RUNTIME index plus a constant delta — the shape the question's tail is written
// with, where the index is a value and the field positions past it are compile-time.
function emitStoreOffsetByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, base ValueId, index ValueId, delta ParsedInt, value ParsedInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let step = emitConst(module, block: block, value: delta, ids: ids)
	let at = emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let byteValue = emitConst(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids)
	emitStoreByte(module, block: block, base: base, index: at, value: byteValue, ids: ids)
end 'emitStoreOffsetByte'

// The four `A`-record bytes as the one word `sin_addr` holds — low byte first, because both are in NETWORK
// order and neither is swapped.
function emitDnsAddressWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, message ValueId, at ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var word = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: message, index: at, ids: ids)
	var scale = 1 as ParsedInt

	for byteIndex in 1 to Ipv4LastOctetIndex 'eachByte'
		scale = scale * Ipv4OctetScale
		let step = emitConst(module, block: block, value: byteIndex, ids: ids)
		let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: at, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
		let loaded = emitLoadByte(module, block: block, base: message, index: index, ids: ids)
		let scaleConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: scale, ids: ids)
		let placed = emitBin(module, block: block, a: loaded, b: scaleConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
		word = emitBin(module, block: block, a: word, b: placed, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	end 'eachByte'

	return word
end 'emitDnsAddressWord'

// ⭐⭐ **`__ms_resolve4(host)` — THE THREE STEPS, IN THE ONLY ORDER THAT WORKS.** A numeric address answers
// without touching the filesystem or the network; `/etc/hosts` answers `localhost` and every private name a
// machine has been told about; DNS answers the rest. `ResolveNoAddress` from all three is what
// `__ms_tcp_connect` turns into `__ManagedSocketError.resolveFailed`.
//
// ⚠ One scratch buffer serves both file reads and is released before the DNS step, which allocates its own —
// so a resolve holds one 8 KiB block at a time rather than two.
function buildManagedSocketResolve(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ManagedSocketResolveName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var numericHit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrnum")
	var hostsStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrh")
	var hostsHit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrhh")
	var serverStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrns")
	var queryStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrq")
	var noAnswer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"msrno")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let host = 0 as ValueId

	let hostLength = ids.mint()
	var measured = emitCStringLength(module, func: func, cur: entry, cstr: host, length: hostLength, ids: ids)

	let numeric = emitResolverCall(module, block: measured, callee: ManagedSocketIpv4Name, args: twoArgs(host, second: hostLength), ids: ids)
	let noAddress = emitConst(module, block: measured, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)
	let isNumeric = emitCmp(module, block: measured, a: numeric, b: noAddress, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(measured, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNumeric, thenBlockId: numericHit.id, elseBlockId: hostsStep.id))

	module.setTerminator(numericHit, op: StdOp.ret(numeric))

	let capacity = emitConst(module, block: hostsStep, value: ResolveTextBytes, ids: ids)
	let text = emitRawAlloc(module, block: hostsStep, byteCount: capacity, ids: ids)
	let fromHosts = emitResolverCall(module, block: hostsStep, callee: ManagedSocketHostsName, args: fourArgs(host, second: hostLength, third: text, fourth: capacity), ids: ids)
	let hostsNoAddress = emitConst(module, block: hostsStep, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)
	let inHosts = emitCmp(module, block: hostsStep, a: fromHosts, b: hostsNoAddress, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(hostsStep, op: StdOp.condBranch(inHosts, thenBlockId: hostsHit.id, elseBlockId: serverStep.id))

	emitRawFree(module, block: hostsHit, ptr: text, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(hostsHit, op: StdOp.ret(fromHosts))

	let server = emitResolverCall(module, block: serverStep, callee: ManagedSocketNameserverName, args: twoArgs(text, second: capacity), ids: ids)
	emitRawFree(module, block: serverStep, ptr: text, ids: ids)
	let serverNoAddress = emitConst(module, block: serverStep, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)
	let haveServer = emitCmp(module, block: serverStep, a: server, b: serverNoAddress, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(serverStep, op: StdOp.condBranch(haveServer, thenBlockId: queryStep.id, elseBlockId: noAnswer.id))

	let queried = emitResolverCall(module, block: queryStep, callee: ManagedSocketDnsName, args: threeArgs(host, second: hostLength, third: server), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(queryStep, op: StdOp.ret(queried))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: noAnswer, value: ResolveNoAddress, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildManagedSocketResolve'
